1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.120
[S01] Hi.

2
00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:04.160
[S01] I guess we get to spend an hour or so together today.

3
00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.929
[S01] And most of the time, I wanted to spend just talking about what you want to talk and answering

4
00:00:07.929 --> 00:00:09.210
[S01] some questions.

5
00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:13.570
[S01] But I thought if you wanted, I'd take about 10 minutes or 15 minutes up front and tell

6
00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:18.429
[S01] you what we're doing at next and why the world might need another computer company.

7
00:00:18.429 --> 00:00:21.929
[S01] Is that something -- do you guys think about these things?

8
00:00:21.929 --> 00:00:23.410
[S01] Okay.

9
00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:27.839
[S01] I thought I'd tell you about some of our mistakes.

10
00:00:27.839 --> 00:00:29.109
[S01] Maybe that would be more useful.

11
00:00:29.109 --> 00:00:34.219
[S01] We have a lot of scar tissue.

12
00:00:34.219 --> 00:00:40.859
[S01] There's a really interesting book that was written by a guy named Paul Strassmann.

13
00:00:40.859 --> 00:00:43.460
[S01] And Paul has one of the more interesting jobs on the planet.

14
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:51.710
[S01] He's this chief information officer, CIO of a very large organization called the Pentagon.

15
00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:53.350
[S01] They really understand software there.

16
00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:56.570
[S01] I had a conversation with him not too long ago, and he said the lesson from the Gulf

17
00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:00.140
[S01] War was that the best software will win the war.

18
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:04.810
[S01] And so they're trying to do a lot of work in the software area.

19
00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:08.719
[S01] He wrote a book, though, before he got this job, called The Business Value of Computers.

20
00:01:08.719 --> 00:01:12.719
[S01] And it's rather thick, and it's not good bedtime reading, but you can plow through it,

21
00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:15.560
[S01] and there's some incredible stuff in it.

22
00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:19.560
[S01] He asked two questions in particular.

23
00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.859
[S01] One was, he surveyed a bunch of companies from not very successful all the way up through

24
00:01:24.859 --> 00:01:26.430
[S01] really successful.

25
00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:28.319
[S01] And there's somebody taking notes here.

26
00:01:28.319 --> 00:01:36.319
[S01] And he asked how much they spent on information technology as a percentage of revenues.

27
00:01:36.319 --> 00:01:38.569
[S01] And he got a very counterintuitive answer, right?

28
00:01:38.569 --> 00:01:42.250
[S01] You'd think that the really successful companies would either spend more or less than the

29
00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:45.019
[S01] not-successful companies, depending on your theory.

30
00:01:45.019 --> 00:01:46.560
[S01] But it was exactly the same.

31
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.739
[S01] They all spent about 2% of revenues on information technology.

32
00:01:50.739 --> 00:01:55.209
[S01] And he found this curious, and so he asked another question: how do they spend their

33
00:01:55.209 --> 00:01:56.030
[S01] money?

34
00:01:56.030 --> 00:02:00.489
[S01] And he found out that the really successful ones -- or actually, let's start with the

35
00:02:00.489 --> 00:02:03.640
[S01] not-so-successful ones.

36
00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:07.489
[S01] As we -- success increases, and dollars increase.

37
00:02:07.489 --> 00:02:13.969
[S01] He found out that the not-so-successful ones spent the majority of their money on management

38
00:02:13.969 --> 00:02:15.469
[S01] productivity.

39
00:02:15.469 --> 00:02:22.259
[S01] And the more successful ones spent the majority of their money on operational productivity

40
00:02:22.259 --> 00:02:24.810
[S01] applications, right?

41
00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.370
[S01] Now, this was not very pleasant for me to read, because I spent the first 10 years of

42
00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:33.120
[S01] my life on management productivity, which was PCs, right?

43
00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.409
[S01] PCs and Macs never attacked operational productivity.

44
00:02:37.409 --> 00:02:39.340
[S01] They just attacked management productivity.

45
00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:40.340
[S01] Why is that?

46
00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:45.759
[S01] Because you can't go down to your local computer store and buy an app that will help you do

47
00:02:45.759 --> 00:02:53.539
[S01] stock trading, or will help you run a hospital, or will help you in whatever operational

48
00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:56.180
[S01] part of your business you want to automate, right?

49
00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.150
[S01] Unless you're a very, very small business, then you could run some accounting packages.

50
00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:02.870
[S01] But other than that, if you were a medium-sized or a large business, these things never attacked

51
00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.620
[S01] operational productivity.

52
00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:13.150
[S01] So we zoom out, and we say, how have people attacked operational productivity with information

53
00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:14.150
[S01] technology?

54
00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:17.340
[S01] Well, in the '60s, they bought a mainframe, and they got some terminals and a bunch of

55
00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:20.009
[S01] COBOL programmers, and they wrote a few apps.

56
00:03:20.009 --> 00:03:23.650
[S01] And most of them were kind of back-room apps.

57
00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.680
[S01] And it sort of worked for the very few that could afford to do this.

58
00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:31.370
[S01] In the '70s, they got a mainframe and some terminals, and they did the same thing.

59
00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.509
[S01] And a few of them got a few mini-computers and terminals and tried to do it a little

60
00:03:34.509 --> 00:03:35.509
[S01] cheaper.

61
00:03:35.509 --> 00:03:37.430
[S01] In the '80s, nothing changed.

62
00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:43.180
[S01] Mainframe and terminals, minis and terminals, until maybe about two or three years ago.

63
00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:49.370
[S01] What happened two or three years ago was that the front office started to realize that

64
00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:57.229
[S01] they needed operational apps so bad that they couldn't depend on the MIS folks anymore.

65
00:03:57.229 --> 00:04:01.590
[S01] And they started taking life into their own hands, and working with -- sometimes working

66
00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:07.680
[S01] with the MIS folks, to start downsizing and getting some servers and running some industry-standard

67
00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.199
[S01] databases like Sybase or Oracle in the servers, and making a little local area network, and

68
00:04:12.199 --> 00:04:17.810
[S01] getting maybe some Sun workstations, and spending about two years writing some mission-critical

69
00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:23.769
[S01] operational applications, like trading apps for Wall Street, perfect example.

70
00:04:23.769 --> 00:04:25.410
[S01] And it kind of worked.

71
00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:30.680
[S01] And the reason that they needed to do this was because more and more they were discovering

72
00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:37.269
[S01] that things like new products required a custom operational application.

73
00:04:37.269 --> 00:04:41.519
[S01] An example: if you're in financial services and you come up with a new product, it's

74
00:04:41.519 --> 00:04:43.329
[S01] only three things.

75
00:04:43.329 --> 00:04:51.000
[S01] It's an idea, it's a sales force, and it's a custom app to bang on databases to make

76
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.829
[S01] the product real, to do the mortgage swaps, or whatever it is you want to do.

77
00:04:54.829 --> 00:04:57.139
[S01] Without the app, you don't have a product.

78
00:04:58.089 --> 00:05:02.310
[S02] And so, there's been a increasing buildup of demand

79
00:05:02.339 --> 00:05:06.350
[S02] from the front parts of corporations

80
00:05:06.379 --> 00:05:11.060
[S02] to create more and more and more of these operational applications.

81
00:05:11.089 --> 00:05:15.029
[S02] And I think it's going to get to the point where

82
00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.800
[S02] this becomes fairly clear that this is the next

83
00:05:18.829 --> 00:05:21.949
[S02] big revolution in desktop computing,

84
00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:26.750
[S02] is to create these, to attack the operational productivity,

85
00:05:26.779 --> 00:05:29.370
[S02] and as we start to re-engineer the way we do things,

86
00:05:29.399 --> 00:05:33.110
[S02] to automate a lot of this in custom applications.

87
00:05:33.139 --> 00:05:34.670
[S02] Sounds a little strange now to most people.

88
00:05:34.699 --> 00:05:37.300
[S02] It sounds like desktop publishing did in 1985.

89
00:05:37.329 --> 00:05:38.459
[S02] Nobody knew what it was.

90
00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:41.329
[S02] Everybody thought it was kind of a strange, vertical thing over there.

91
00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.290
[S02] But my guess is it's pretty horizontal.

92
00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:47.980
[S02] And we're attacking vertical markets now

93
00:05:48.009 --> 00:05:50.850
[S02] that know they want this.

94
00:05:50.879 --> 00:05:52.350
[S02] And it's going extremely well.

95
00:05:52.379 --> 00:05:56.230
[S02] Sun is the only company that's really had any success at this.

96
00:05:56.259 --> 00:05:59.089
[S02] And we're knocking them out of the box

97
00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.329
[S02] because we came up with this software called NextStep,

98
00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:05.470
[S02] which lets you build apps five to 10 times faster

99
00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:07.529
[S02] than anything anyone's ever seen.

100
00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:08.639
[S02] And after you build them,

101
00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:10.699
[S02] they're deployable and usable by mere mortals

102
00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:12.939
[S02] because it's really easy to use, this computer.

103
00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:15.779
[S02] And you can interoperate your custom apps seamlessly

104
00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:17.959
[S02] with a bunch of off-the-shelf productivity apps.

105
00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:21.329
[S02] So, we go to these companies that use Suns

106
00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.129
[S02] and take two years to write their apps,

107
00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:24.470
[S02] or are thinking about using Suns,

108
00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:27.670
[S02] and they can write their apps in about 90 days on a Next.

109
00:06:27.699 --> 00:06:29.430
[S02] Now, if you're on Wall Street

110
00:06:29.459 --> 00:06:32.839
[S02] and you can write, you can create a new product in 90 days

111
00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:34.300
[S02] versus your competitor in two years,

112
00:06:34.329 --> 00:06:38.199
[S02] that's eight new products you can field for their every one.

113
00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:40.540
[S02] And you can start to see the competitive advantage

114
00:06:40.569 --> 00:06:42.930
[S02] that can be created this way.

115
00:06:42.959 --> 00:06:45.939
[S02] Now, we had no idea that we were any good at this

116
00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:49.009
[S02] when we started Next.

117
00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.769
[S02] A lot of times, you don't know

118
00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.129
[S02] what your competitive advantage is

119
00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:57.100
[S02] when you launch a new product.

120
00:06:57.129 --> 00:06:58.339
[S02] Let me give you a historical example.

121
00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:01.370
[S02] When we created them, how many of you guys use Macs?

122
00:07:01.399 --> 00:07:04.100
[S02] Good.

123
00:07:04.129 --> 00:07:06.740
[S02] How many of you have seen a Next?

124
00:07:06.769 --> 00:07:09.519
[S02] Oh, that's, how many of you use a Next?

125
00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:13.050
[S02] Oh, that's not so bad.

126
00:07:13.079 --> 00:07:16.290
[S02] I would like to change that ratio.

127
00:07:16.319 --> 00:07:19.149
[S02] We're on the right track, anyway.

128
00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:22.600
[S02] When we started, when we did the Macintosh,

129
00:07:22.629 --> 00:07:24.930
[S02] we never anticipated desktop publishing

130
00:07:24.959 --> 00:07:26.930
[S02] when we created the Mac.

131
00:07:26.959 --> 00:07:28.170
[S02] Sounds funny

132
00:07:28.199 --> 00:07:31.269
[S02] because that turned out to be the Mac's compelling advantage, right?

133
00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:33.470
[S02] The thing that it did, not one and a half

134
00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:34.800
[S02] or two times better than everything else,

135
00:07:34.829 --> 00:07:37.069
[S02] but you know, four or five times better than anything else,

136
00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:39.009
[S02] where you had to have one.

137
00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:40.579
[S02] We never anticipated it.

138
00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:42.879
[S02] We anticipated bitmap displays and laser printers,

139
00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:44.519
[S02] but we never thought about page maker

140
00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:47.050
[S02] or that whole industry really coming down to the desktop.

141
00:07:47.079 --> 00:07:49.449
[S02] Maybe we weren't smart enough.

142
00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:50.850
[S02] But we were smart enough

143
00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:55.089
[S02] to see it start to happen nine to 12 months later.

144
00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:59.129
[S02] And we changed our entire marketing and business strategy

145
00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:01.360
[S02] to focus on desktop publishing.

146
00:08:01.389 --> 00:08:03.000
[S02] And it became the Trojan horse

147
00:08:03.029 --> 00:08:05.230
[S02] that eventually got the Mac into corporate America,

148
00:08:05.259 --> 00:08:06.399
[S02] where it could show its owners

149
00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:09.269
[S02] all the other wonderful things it could do.

150
00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:13.000
[S02] Likewise, when we created NextStep,

151
00:08:13.029 --> 00:08:16.899
[S02] this revolutionary, object-oriented software that we have,

152
00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:20.199
[S02] our target customer coming from the PC world,

153
00:08:20.230 --> 00:08:22.600
[S02] where shrink-wrapped apps were king,

154
00:08:22.629 --> 00:08:24.699
[S02] was Lotus and Adobe and WordPerfect

155
00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:26.629
[S02] and all the shrink-wrapped apps developers.

156
00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:29.170
[S02] And the purpose was to let them create their apps

157
00:08:29.199 --> 00:08:31.139
[S02] five to 10 times faster, these shrink-wrapped apps.

158
00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:31.639
[S02] And it worked.

159
00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:33.240
[S02] We have a ton of shrink-wrapped apps now,

160
00:08:33.269 --> 00:08:36.299
[S02] best of breed in almost every category.

161
00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:39.870
[S02] But it wasn't until early in '91,

162
00:08:39.899 --> 00:08:42.169
[S02] early last year, a little over a year ago,

163
00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:44.600
[S02] that some really big companies came to us and said,

164
00:08:44.629 --> 00:08:47.169
[S02] "You don't understand what you've got."

165
00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:50.299
[S02] The same software that allows Lotus

166
00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:52.870
[S02] to create their apps five to 10 times faster

167
00:09:54.049 --> 00:09:55.909
[S03] It will be for them, but they won't see.

168
00:09:59.309 --> 00:10:02.029
[S03] I think it's gone past the point where it's possible.

169
00:10:02.029 --> 00:10:07.269
[S03] And they can't, the greatest thing is hardware is really, hardware churns every 18 months.

170
00:10:07.269 --> 00:10:13.929
[S03] It's pretty impossible to get a sustainable competitive advantage from hardware.

171
00:10:13.929 --> 00:10:18.269
[S03] If you're lucky, you can make something one and a half or two times as good as your competitor,

172
00:10:18.269 --> 00:10:21.570
[S03] which probably isn't enough to be quite a competitive advantage.

173
00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:24.200
[S03] And it only lasts for six months.

174
00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:29.330
[S03] But software seems to take a lot longer for people to catch up with.

175
00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:33.940
[S03] I watched Microsoft take eight or nine years to catch up with the Mac, and it's arguable

176
00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:36.210
[S03] whether they've even caught up.

177
00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:37.980
[S03] It takes a long time.

178
00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:42.019
[S03] And we think that the soonest we're going to have a true competitor is probably four

179
00:10:42.019 --> 00:10:43.610
[S03] to five years.

180
00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:49.570
[S03] So we've got that amount of time to grow ourselves a one to two billion dollar company

181
00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:51.500
[S03] so that we can compete with them on scale.

182
00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:53.929
[S03] See, today, we can't compete with them on scale.

183
00:10:53.929 --> 00:10:56.799
[S03] We never have as many salespeople as they do, as Sun does.

184
00:10:56.799 --> 00:10:58.799
[S03] We don't have the ad budgets that they do.

185
00:10:58.799 --> 00:11:01.289
[S03] So we've got to have a better product.

186
00:11:01.289 --> 00:11:05.470
[S03] Assuming, and I hope we always have a better product, and I think we can, but I'd also

187
00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:08.210
[S03] like to be able to at least give them a run for their money on scale.

188
00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:13.379
[S03] So we've got the next three to four years to run really fast so that by the time they

189
00:11:13.379 --> 00:11:17.279
[S03] even get close to having a competitive product, we're at a large enough scale to where we

190
00:11:17.279 --> 00:11:19.899
[S03] can start to compete with them.

191
00:11:19.899 --> 00:11:22.610
[S03] And that's what we're doing with our lives right now.

192
00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:28.710
[S03] Spending a lot of time with customers, spending a lot of time making next step better, and

193
00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:30.649
[S03] that kind of thing.

194
00:11:30.649 --> 00:11:33.070
[S03] So that's the strategic basis of what we do.

195
00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:38.710
[S03] Does that make any sense to you?

196
00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:44.110
[S03] Have you run across the concept of sort of operational custom applications at all?

197
00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:47.000
[S03] I mean, you guys, most of you have come from companies where you've had work experience,

198
00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:48.000
[S03] right?

199
00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:49.139
[S03] And you've all done that.

200
00:11:49.139 --> 00:11:53.350
[S03] So do you have this problem in the companies you've come from of a lot of pressure to

201
00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:58.039
[S03] write these operational custom applications and hardly anything coming out of the spigot

202
00:11:58.039 --> 00:12:00.009
[S03] to satisfy this thirst?

203
00:12:00.009 --> 00:12:02.450
[S03] Yeah.

204
00:12:02.450 --> 00:12:05.200
[S03] How many of you from Wall Street?

205
00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:06.200
[S03] Good.

206
00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:08.629
[S03] Good.

207
00:12:08.629 --> 00:12:11.700
[S03] How many of you are from manufacturing companies?

208
00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:12.850
[S03] Oh, excellent.

209
00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:15.169
[S03] Where are the rest of you from?

210
00:12:18.919 --> 00:12:20.980
[S03] Oh, how many from consulting?

211
00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:21.980
[S03] Oh, that's bad.

212
00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:29.230
[S03] A mind is too important to waste.

213
00:12:32.669 --> 00:12:33.830
[S03] You should do something.

214
00:12:48.769 --> 00:12:51.799
[S03] The only consultants I've seen that I think are truly useful are the ones that help us

215
00:12:51.799 --> 00:12:53.269
[S03] sell our computers.

216
00:12:53.269 --> 00:12:57.990
[S03] But other than that, no, seriously, I don't think there's anything inherently evil in

217
00:12:57.990 --> 00:12:58.990
[S03] consulting.

218
00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:09.769
[S03] I think that, I think that without, without owning something over an extended period

219
00:13:09.769 --> 00:13:15.659
[S03] of time, like a few years, where one has a chance to take responsibility for one's

220
00:13:15.659 --> 00:13:21.440
[S03] recommendations, where one has to see one's recommendations through all action stages

221
00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:25.600
[S03] and accumulate scar tissue for the mistakes and pick oneself up off the ground and dust

222
00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:33.990
[S03] oneself off, one learns a fraction of what one can.

223
00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:41.350
[S03] Coming in and making recommendations and not owning the results, not owning the implementation,

224
00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:49.740
[S03] I think is, is a fraction of the value and a fraction of the opportunity to learn and

225
00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:51.220
[S03] get better.

226
00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:57.769
[S03] And so what you, you do get a broad cut at companies, but it's very thin.

227
00:13:57.769 --> 00:14:02.470
[S03] It's like a picture of a, I could use, I'm a vegetarian, so I wouldn't use steak, but

228
00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:07.100
[S03] it's like a picture of a, of a banana.

229
00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:11.919
[S03] You might get a very accurate picture, but it's only two dimensional.

230
00:14:11.919 --> 00:14:18.059
[S03] And without the experience of actually doing it, you never get three dimensional.

231
00:14:18.059 --> 00:14:19.779
[S03] So you might have a lot of pictures on your walls.

232
00:14:19.779 --> 00:14:21.059
[S03] You can show it off to your friends.

233
00:14:21.059 --> 00:14:25.230
[S03] You can say, look, I've worked in bananas, I've worked in peaches, I've worked in grapes,

234
00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:28.120
[S03] but you never really taste it.

235
00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:31.909
[S03] And, and I think that, that's, that's what I think.

236
00:14:41.059 --> 00:14:44.370
[S03] You're also a variable expense, and in hard times, you find yourself.

237
00:14:47.679 --> 00:14:49.480
[S03] You find yourself variable, right?

238
00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:50.480
[S03] Yes?

239
00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:54.059
[S04] If it's the software that's going to make you break your company, how come you're

240
00:14:54.059 --> 00:14:55.059
[S04] putting it on a platform?

241
00:14:55.059 --> 00:14:55.559
[S04] Why don't you?

242
00:14:57.500 --> 00:15:00.879
[S05] Right, very good question. I'm going to generalize your question.

243
00:15:00.879 --> 00:15:05.919
[S05] Why don't we just become a software company, right?

244
00:15:05.919 --> 00:15:09.620
[S05] Some very good questions, the subtle question. I'm going to try to go through some several

245
00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:14.490
[S05] things and I'm sorry if I jump around.

246
00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:19.559
[S05] We got a lot of requests from customers last year that they would love to see Next

247
00:15:19.559 --> 00:15:26.620
[S05] Step on other platforms and primarily Intel-based platforms like the 486.

248
00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:31.799
[S05] So we decided to do just that and we have ported Next Step to the 486 and we're finishing

249
00:15:31.799 --> 00:15:36.220
[S05] it now and it'll ship in the September, October timeframe.

250
00:15:36.220 --> 00:15:39.740
[S05] And it's exactly the same stuff we run on our own computer.

251
00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:45.200
[S05] And same apps, same user interface, same training, same development environment.

252
00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:49.860
[S05] And we're going to sell it for $9.95 and we're OEMing it to a bunch of companies whose names

253
00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:54.950
[S05] you'll recognize quite easily and OEMing it to them at a much cheaper price.

254
00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:59.820
[S05] And everybody's coming out of the woodwork to help us.

255
00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:03.690
[S05] We're getting help from Novell, we're getting help from all the developers, Intel's really

256
00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:05.320
[S05] helping us.

257
00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:07.070
[S05] And they really want us to succeed.

258
00:16:07.070 --> 00:16:09.230
[S05] Why is that?

259
00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:10.320
[S05] Right.

260
00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:15.120
[S05] They all want to make sure that there's a choice out there and they're all really scared

261
00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:16.820
[S05] about Microsoft.

262
00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:20.870
[S05] And they see Next Step as the only thing on the horizon that can challenge Microsoft

263
00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:23.620
[S05] in system software for the next several years.

264
00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:28.620
[S05] So we're enjoying a lot of help and, you know, boy, we need it, so that's good.

265
00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:33.580
[S05] Now, we've also had a lot of requests from companies to port Next Step to other platforms

266
00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:36.370
[S05] and we're talking to some of those companies right now.

267
00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:40.330
[S05] Now Sun, we got a lot of requests from Sun customers to port Next Step to Sun.

268
00:16:40.330 --> 00:16:42.980
[S05] So a lot of them are saying, "Look, we may not want to buy them anymore, but we already

269
00:16:42.980 --> 00:16:44.759
[S05] bought 500 and we don't want to throw them in the bay.

270
00:16:44.759 --> 00:16:46.039
[S05] So can we put your software on them?"

271
00:16:46.039 --> 00:16:48.330
[S05] Because Sun's falling behind in software.

272
00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:54.370
[S05] Now, Sun says they'd rather stick needles in their eyes than help us do this.

273
00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:56.690
[S05] That's a quote.

274
00:16:56.690 --> 00:17:07.140
[S05] And so we're evaluating right now which will be worse for Sun, if we port it or if we

275
00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:08.670
[S05] don't port it.

276
00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:14.099
[S05] And since we're fairly customer-driven, we'll probably end up doing what the customers

277
00:17:14.099 --> 00:17:17.380
[S05] ask us to do, because we want to make them happy.

278
00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:22.519
[S05] Now, this leads one into the question, well, this leads one into the question, should

279
00:17:22.519 --> 00:17:23.920
[S05] we just be a software company?

280
00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:26.230
[S05] And we think the answer is no.

281
00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:30.630
[S05] We think we should be a software company and a hardware company.

282
00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:37.390
[S05] In making the decision to put Next Step on more than our own platforms, we clearly decided

283
00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:41.519
[S05] that we will sell less than 100% of the Next Step hardware.

284
00:17:41.519 --> 00:17:48.019
[S05] However, we think that the marketplace will grow and we will sell more absolute hardware.

285
00:17:48.019 --> 00:17:52.819
[S05] And secondly, the charter of our hardware division is to make the best Next Step hardware.

286
00:17:52.819 --> 00:17:55.849
[S05] Might not be the cheapest, might not be this, might not be that, but we think all in all

287
00:17:55.849 --> 00:17:57.769
[S05] we can make the best stuff.

288
00:17:57.769 --> 00:18:03.319
[S05] And I would love it nothing better than if someday we only sold 20 or 25% of the Next

289
00:18:03.319 --> 00:18:04.519
[S05] Step hardware.

290
00:18:04.519 --> 00:18:09.130
[S05] But I still think that's a billion-dollar-plus hardware business.

291
00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:11.990
[S05] And I'll get into one other hardware-related reason in a minute.

292
00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:25.490
[S05] There's some things I can't talk about here, but it's...

293
00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:34.960
[S05] In addition to that, if you look at how we sell our computers right now, we have a sales

294
00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:40.849
[S05] force in the US of about 130 professionals in the field out selling Next Computers.

295
00:18:40.849 --> 00:18:49.319
[S05] They spend 90% of their time selling Next Step software and then 10% of their time

296
00:18:49.319 --> 00:18:51.019
[S05] selling the hardware.

297
00:18:51.019 --> 00:18:55.910
[S05] In other words, if they can get the customer to buy into Next Step, then they're going

298
00:18:55.910 --> 00:18:59.740
[S05] to sell the hardware, because right now we have the only hardware it runs on.

299
00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:03.910
[S05] So they are out there selling Next Step right now.

300
00:19:03.910 --> 00:19:09.319
[S05] And this is what is required to launch a new innovative product.

301
00:19:09.319 --> 00:19:14.730
[S05] The current distribution channels for the computer industry over the last several years

302
00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:18.630
[S05] have lost their ability to create demand.

303
00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:21.529
[S05] They can fulfill demand, but they can't create it.

304
00:19:21.529 --> 00:19:25.210
[S05] If a new product comes out, you're lucky if you can find somebody at the computer store

305
00:19:25.210 --> 00:19:28.339
[S05] that even knows how to demo it, right?

306
00:19:28.339 --> 00:19:33.759
[S05] So the more innovative the product is, the more revolutionary it is and not just an incremental

307
00:19:33.759 --> 00:19:39.460
[S05] improvement, the more you're stuck, because the existing channels only fulfilling demand.

308
00:19:39.460 --> 00:19:43.450
[S05] Matter of fact, it's getting so bad that it's getting wiped out, because there are

309
00:19:43.450 --> 00:19:48.890
[S05] more efficient channels to fulfill demand, like the telephone and Federal Express.

310
00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:54.339
[S05] So we're seeing that the channel become condensed on its way to, I think, just telebusiness.

311
00:19:55.309 --> 00:19:58.819
[S06] So how does one bring innovation to the marketplace?

312
00:19:58.819 --> 00:20:03.140
[S06] We believe the only way we know how to do it right now is with a direct sales force,

313
00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:07.400
[S06] out there in front of customers, showing them the products in the environment of their

314
00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:12.599
[S06] own problems and discussing how those problems can be mated with these solutions.

315
00:20:12.599 --> 00:20:18.049
[S06] A software-only company could never afford to field a direct sales force.

316
00:20:18.049 --> 00:20:24.799
[S06] With average selling prices of $500 a software package, you could never afford 130 professionals

317
00:20:24.799 --> 00:20:27.319
[S06] in the field.

318
00:20:27.319 --> 00:20:32.390
[S06] With an average selling price of $5,000, you can.

319
00:20:32.390 --> 00:20:39.759
[S06] And that's why I don't think we're going to see any more system-software companies succeed.

320
00:20:39.759 --> 00:20:46.130
[S06] I don't think it's possible to fund the effort to educate the market about a revolutionary

321
00:20:46.130 --> 00:20:49.470
[S06] product with ASPs that low.

322
00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:54.099
[S06] And if it's not a revolutionary product, I don't think the company can succeed.

323
00:20:54.099 --> 00:21:00.210
[S06] So our strategy has been that we've got to be a hardware company in order to make our

324
00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:02.390
[S06] software business succeed.

325
00:21:02.390 --> 00:21:03.920
[S06] And we think we can do really well at both of them.

326
00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:09.390
[S06] I know that's a long answer, but it's a complex problem, too.

327
00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:10.390
[S06] Yes?

328
00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:17.589
[S07] You mentioned about the software companies that have a revolutionary product.

329
00:21:17.589 --> 00:21:22.599
[S07] IBM, Apple, and Taligent supposedly have the running.

330
00:21:22.599 --> 00:21:26.890
[S07] And your answer is pretty straightforward on how you feel about it, but I wondered if

331
00:21:26.890 --> 00:21:31.000
[S07] you could deal with your group feelings with Taligent also, or what?

332
00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:33.450
[S06] Sure.

333
00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:38.319
[S06] If you ask us who our competitors are, we'll say really three things.

334
00:21:38.319 --> 00:21:43.319
[S06] One is Sun's Solaris software, the other is Microsoft, and the third is Taligent.

335
00:21:43.319 --> 00:21:45.390
[S06] Let's take them in that order.

336
00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:52.309
[S06] Sun, for a while, had a software value added because they had the best Unix in the marketplace.

337
00:21:52.309 --> 00:21:55.809
[S06] But the markets moved way beyond that, and unfortunately, Sun hasn't.

338
00:21:55.809 --> 00:21:58.490
[S06] So their software's falling further and further behind.

339
00:21:58.490 --> 00:22:03.089
[S06] And while we take them very seriously, we don't think Solaris is going to be much competition.

340
00:22:03.089 --> 00:22:09.789
[S06] It's not, doesn't have an object in it, and it's pretty much what they have today.

341
00:22:09.789 --> 00:22:14.970
[S06] Microsoft is doing NT, which is their sort of second attempt at a Unix wannabe.

342
00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:18.910
[S06] And that's great, and I think it'll be better than the last one, which was OS2.

343
00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:21.539
[S06] But fundamentally, it's just an operating system.

344
00:22:21.539 --> 00:22:23.250
[S06] It's better plumbing for Windows.

345
00:22:23.250 --> 00:22:25.250
[S06] It's a good way of thinking of it.

346
00:22:25.250 --> 00:22:31.250
[S06] Unfortunately, you're still stuck with Windows, in all of its glory, including the worst

347
00:22:31.250 --> 00:22:34.000
[S06] development environment that's ever been invented.

348
00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:38.500
[S06] And so we don't think that this is really going to present a challenge to what we're

349
00:22:38.500 --> 00:22:42.589
[S06] going after, which are these mission-critical custom apps, because the development environment

350
00:22:42.589 --> 00:22:46.619
[S06] is horrendous, it's not object-oriented, and even with the better plumbing, we think

351
00:22:46.619 --> 00:22:52.460
[S06] it will be widely rejected for what we do.

352
00:22:52.460 --> 00:22:58.150
[S06] In terms of Taligent, Taligent represents the first true competitor that we could have.

353
00:22:58.150 --> 00:23:04.119
[S06] They're going to ship a product around 1995 if they execute to their plans.

354
00:23:04.119 --> 00:23:09.460
[S06] And I think if they do execute to their plans and work really hard, by about the mid-'90s,

355
00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:12.089
[S06] they'll have roughly what we have today.

356
00:23:12.089 --> 00:23:13.930
[S06] And that's not meant as a joke.

357
00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:17.880
[S06] It still means we have to run very hard, because they will have a lot of resources

358
00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:19.539
[S06] at their disposal.

359
00:23:19.539 --> 00:23:22.789
[S06] I think there's a lot of questions as to whether they will ever ship a product.

360
00:23:22.789 --> 00:23:26.960
[S06] I think they're a few years away from having something running, unquote.

361
00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:28.900
[S06] But we take them very seriously.

362
00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:34.180
[S06] Now, they've helped us enormously, because they've blessed object-oriented programming.

363
00:23:34.180 --> 00:23:37.819
[S06] And right now, we're the only folks that have it, and will be for the next three, four

364
00:23:37.819 --> 00:23:38.900
[S06] years.

365
00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:43.980
[S06] So if we can't compete with Taligent, it's probably because we've shot ourselves in

366
00:23:43.980 --> 00:23:45.089
[S06] the foot.

367
00:23:45.089 --> 00:23:46.789
[S06] You couldn't ask for something better.

368
00:23:46.789 --> 00:23:52.539
[S06] IBM and Apple saying, "These guys are right on track, and come back in five years and

369
00:23:52.539 --> 00:23:54.730
[S06] we'll have it too."

370
00:23:54.730 --> 00:23:55.900
[S06] It's great.

371
00:23:55.900 --> 00:23:58.509
[S06] And so the ball's in our court.

372
00:23:58.509 --> 00:23:59.900
[S06] Yeah.

373
00:23:59.900 --> 00:24:03.319
[S06] Yes, in the back.

374
00:24:03.319 --> 00:24:08.650
[S07] You've described NextStep as an environment and an operating system, right?

375
00:24:08.650 --> 00:24:11.920
[S07] And what I hear you talking about is this great way to develop applications.

376
00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:15.660
[S07] And I don't develop applications, I use other operating systems.

377
00:24:15.660 --> 00:24:21.900
[S07] Do you see, I mean, a fracture of the operating system market into a specialized niche that

378
00:24:21.900 --> 00:24:25.609
[S07] you're describing, and then the general market for those of us who don't develop apps?

379
00:24:25.609 --> 00:24:29.400
[S07] Or are you saying that you can bridge from an environment that allows you to develop

380
00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:35.220
[S07] apps into an operating system that'll be like Windows or MS-DOS on a lot of machines?

381
00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:37.460
[S06] Well, let's get our terminology straight.

382
00:24:37.460 --> 00:24:43.109
[S06] We look at NextStep as an operating environment, much more than an operating system.

383
00:24:43.109 --> 00:24:47.799
[S06] Unix, which is our operating system, is 10% of NextStep.

384
00:24:47.799 --> 00:24:55.109
[S06] So in these modern operating environments, when you develop a custom app in NextStep,

385
00:24:55.109 --> 00:24:57.549
[S06] it doesn't run on the Mac or on Windows.

386
00:24:58.349 --> 00:25:02.750
[S08] Can't. You need all the objects that come with NextStep to make it work.

387
00:25:02.750 --> 00:25:04.440
[S08] And let me go into a little detail there.

388
00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:07.230
[S08] How many of you are technical here?

389
00:25:07.230 --> 00:25:08.759
[S08] A lot. Okay, great.

390
00:25:08.759 --> 00:25:11.470
[S08] Well, we've discovered something.

391
00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:17.460
[S08] You don't write code any faster in NextStep than you do in any other operating and development

392
00:25:17.460 --> 00:25:18.960
[S08] environment that we know of.

393
00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:27.369
[S08] However, to do a particular app on average, you write about 20% of the code that you do

394
00:25:27.369 --> 00:25:30.109
[S08] in any other development environment we know of.

395
00:25:30.109 --> 00:25:34.859
[S08] About 20% of the code than in Sun or in anything else.

396
00:25:34.859 --> 00:25:41.390
[S08] So, the code that's the fastest to write, the code that's the easiest to maintain, and

397
00:25:41.390 --> 00:25:44.460
[S08] the code that never breaks is the code you don't write.

398
00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:45.829
[S08] So that's our strategy.

399
00:25:45.829 --> 00:25:47.890
[S08] Write a lot less code.

400
00:25:47.890 --> 00:25:55.079
[S08] And one of the ways, the way we do this is we enable the developer to use a lot of objects

401
00:25:55.079 --> 00:25:56.279
[S08] that others have written.

402
00:25:56.279 --> 00:25:59.579
[S08] We ship six years' worth of objects with NextStep.

403
00:25:59.579 --> 00:26:03.599
[S08] You can create your own objects for your own company and then reuse them around your

404
00:26:03.599 --> 00:26:04.599
[S08] developers.

405
00:26:04.599 --> 00:26:08.740
[S08] And there's now independent third-party companies not selling system software, not selling

406
00:26:08.740 --> 00:26:11.250
[S08] applications, but selling objects.

407
00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:15.130
[S08] There's about a, not quite 10 of them, and it's starting.

408
00:26:15.130 --> 00:26:17.410
[S08] And I think it's going to be a very big thing.

409
00:26:17.410 --> 00:26:23.069
[S08] So, in order to deliver these apps that are created on NextStep, you have to have NextStep

410
00:26:23.069 --> 00:26:27.740
[S08] so they can run on top and take advantage of this rich community of objects.

411
00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:30.359
[S08] And that's why we're porting NextStep to the 486.

412
00:26:30.359 --> 00:26:34.430
[S08] That's why you'll see NextStep running on several hardware platforms.

413
00:26:34.430 --> 00:26:39.839
[S08] And will there be a fracturing, if you will?

414
00:26:39.839 --> 00:26:40.839
[S08] Sure.

415
00:26:40.839 --> 00:26:45.069
[S08] To some extent, there will, and I tend to look at it as a transition.

416
00:26:45.069 --> 00:26:47.430
[S08] Was there a fracturing when Mac came out?

417
00:26:47.430 --> 00:26:48.640
[S08] Yes.

418
00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:54.950
[S08] And there's been more of a transition as people either move to Mac or, in the case

419
00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:58.640
[S08] of Windows, adopt what's good about the Mac.

420
00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:00.529
[S08] Same thing's going to happen here.

421
00:27:00.529 --> 00:27:04.430
[S08] We believe very strongly that the benefits from these object-oriented environments, not

422
00:27:04.430 --> 00:27:07.599
[S08] only just rapid development, but a much richer user environment.

423
00:27:07.599 --> 00:27:11.539
[S08] If you use a Next, it's a lot nicer than a Mac or a PC, even if you've never developed

424
00:27:11.539 --> 00:27:13.440
[S08] an app.

425
00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:19.230
[S08] Other environments will absorb some of those breakthroughs, and more and more people will

426
00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:20.440
[S08] use NextStep.

427
00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:23.789
[S08] And things will tend to balance themselves out over time.

428
00:27:23.789 --> 00:27:25.730
[S08] But there will be a transition.

429
00:27:25.730 --> 00:27:29.009
[S08] And our goal is to make sure we're part of it.

430
00:27:29.009 --> 00:27:30.009
[S08] Yeah?

431
00:27:30.009 --> 00:27:36.660
[S09] You spoke earlier that there was a competitive advantage you felt lied in the fact that

432
00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:41.970
[S09] these companies today were needing development in kind of the operational basis as opposed

433
00:27:41.970 --> 00:27:44.799
[S09] to the management side.

434
00:27:44.799 --> 00:27:48.690
[S09] Many companies, though, or some perhaps, outsource their development.

435
00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:51.190
[S09] How does this change your business strategy?

436
00:27:51.190 --> 00:27:52.549
[S08] Good question.

437
00:27:52.549 --> 00:27:58.579
[S08] If our business strategy says that we do a lot of things well, but the tip of our arrow

438
00:27:58.579 --> 00:28:03.190
[S08] or our Trojan horse, which is getting us into these large and medium-sized accounts,

439
00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:09.130
[S08] is our custom application development ability, then our growth is going to be paced, among

440
00:28:09.130 --> 00:28:14.829
[S08] other things, by the development community available to these companies to develop these

441
00:28:14.829 --> 00:28:16.660
[S08] apps, right?

442
00:28:16.660 --> 00:28:22.630
[S08] Now, even though we have shrunk the development time down to a fraction of what it was, still,

443
00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:25.829
[S08] without developers out there, we're not going to win.

444
00:28:25.829 --> 00:28:36.220
[S08] Fortunately, most companies have really increased the staffs of good people in their IS departments

445
00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:39.190
[S08] dramatically in the last several years, last four or five years.

446
00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:44.299
[S08] There's now really bright people who know something about computer science in IS departments.

447
00:28:44.299 --> 00:28:48.720
[S08] And most of the industries we talk to, whether they're healthcare, financial services, even

448
00:28:48.720 --> 00:28:53.440
[S08] law enforcement, places like that, have onsite developers in their IS teams.

449
00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:58.609
[S08] If not, they're starting to become a lot of VARs and people out there that we're using

450
00:28:58.609 --> 00:29:02.079
[S08] in LA or in New York, where just was, we probably have about 10 independent third-party

451
00:29:02.079 --> 00:29:05.190
[S08] companies helping parts of Wall Street develop things.

452
00:29:05.190 --> 00:29:09.579
[S08] So, I think that's not going to be a stumbling block.

453
00:29:09.579 --> 00:29:13.440
[S08] My personal opinion is the number of people in applications development is shrinking.

454
00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:18.380
[S08] It exploded in the 80s with PCs, but now that industry's consolidating.

455
00:29:18.380 --> 00:29:23.839
[S08] It's consolidating down to very few companies, and there's a lot of excess people out there

456
00:29:23.839 --> 00:29:29.789
[S08] that I think are starting to get channeled into some of these other areas.

457
00:29:29.789 --> 00:29:33.019
[S08] So far, it hasn't been a problem, but if, you know, if you want to do that, please

458
00:29:33.019 --> 00:29:35.230
[S08] call us and tell us because we always need more.

459
00:29:35.230 --> 00:29:37.650
[S08] So, what are you guys all going to do when you get out of here?

460
00:29:37.650 --> 00:29:38.940
[S08] You going to go back to your companies?

461
00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:40.339
[S08] Is that?

462
00:29:40.339 --> 00:29:42.900
[S08] How many are going to go back?

463
00:29:42.900 --> 00:29:45.339
[S08] How many aren't going to go back?

464
00:29:45.339 --> 00:29:48.940
[S08] What are you going to do?

465
00:29:56.420 --> 00:29:57.720
[S10] You know, technically-based people.

466
00:29:57.720 --> 00:30:00.710
[S10] And you guys are getting a great business education here.

467
00:30:00.710 --> 00:30:03.009
[S10] Yeah, we'd love to talk to you.

468
00:30:04.309 --> 00:30:05.640
[S10] Yeah, yes, sir.

469
00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:07.869
[S11] I don't want to step in any toes here, but

470
00:30:07.869 --> 00:30:08.369
[S10] Go ahead.

471
00:30:08.369 --> 00:30:10.150
[S11] I think Apple would be, had you not left it.

472
00:30:10.150 --> 00:30:14.339
[S11] And specifically, could it come out with something revolutionary like that?

473
00:30:15.750 --> 00:30:17.309
[S10] Well, these are deep questions.

474
00:30:22.190 --> 00:30:25.609
[S10] I tell you, I've obviously thought about this a lot.

475
00:30:25.609 --> 00:30:33.269
[S10] And I don't want to get into it too much, but I will say that I think everybody lost.

476
00:30:33.269 --> 00:30:34.269
[S10] I think I lost.

477
00:30:34.269 --> 00:30:36.309
[S10] And I wanted to spend my life there.

478
00:30:36.309 --> 00:30:38.180
[S10] I think Apple lost.

479
00:30:38.180 --> 00:30:41.210
[S10] I think customers lost.

480
00:30:41.210 --> 00:30:44.980
[S10] And having said all that, so what?

481
00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:45.980
[S10] You go on.

482
00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:48.019
[S10] You know, it's not as bad as a lot of things.

483
00:30:48.019 --> 00:30:50.410
[S10] It's not as bad as, you know, losing your arm.

484
00:30:52.019 --> 00:30:55.839
[S10] So, people go on, and companies go on.

485
00:30:55.839 --> 00:30:59.589
[S10] And I think Apple, I'm very happy every time Apple ships a Mac.

486
00:30:59.589 --> 00:31:01.309
[S10] Makes me very, very happy.

487
00:31:01.309 --> 00:31:03.789
[S10] I think the Power Books are decent products.

488
00:31:03.789 --> 00:31:06.150
[S10] I like them.

489
00:31:06.150 --> 00:31:08.430
[S10] But Apple has been struggling the last few years.

490
00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:11.509
[S10] They've been having a real struggle with who they want to be.

491
00:31:11.509 --> 00:31:12.759
[S10] And this is nothing new.

492
00:31:12.759 --> 00:31:14.089
[S10] We always had that.

493
00:31:14.089 --> 00:31:17.289
[S10] That was part of what kept Apple alive, I think.

494
00:31:17.289 --> 00:31:19.059
[S10] And there were two camps within Apple.

495
00:31:19.059 --> 00:31:23.269
[S10] Camp one wanted to be the next serious computer company.

496
00:31:23.269 --> 00:31:27.880
[S10] And camp two wanted to sort of be the Sony of computers.

497
00:31:27.880 --> 00:31:32.509
[S10] And that struggling, I think, was somewhat tearing Apple apart.

498
00:31:32.509 --> 00:31:35.619
[S10] And fortunately, the Sony guys have won.

499
00:31:35.619 --> 00:31:38.380
[S10] They've kind of decided to go be the Sony of computers.

500
00:31:38.380 --> 00:31:42.119
[S10] And so the Power Books are pretty good, but the Quadras, you know, suck wind right now,

501
00:31:42.119 --> 00:31:43.200
[S10] the high-end stuff.

502
00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:45.519
[S10] And they're abdigating.

503
00:31:45.519 --> 00:31:51.559
[S10] They're basically not putting a lot of resources onto the power users on desktops.

504
00:31:51.559 --> 00:31:56.670
[S10] And they've put most of their best people now on the portables and on consumer products

505
00:31:56.670 --> 00:31:58.329
[S10] that they're going to be coming out with.

506
00:31:58.329 --> 00:32:00.269
[S10] And I think they'll do very well at that.

507
00:32:00.269 --> 00:32:07.450
[S10] Now, there's a problem in there, in that if you look at the consumer products that sell

508
00:32:07.450 --> 00:32:12.539
[S10] over a million a year, you can count them on a few hands, right?

509
00:32:12.539 --> 00:32:15.569
[S10] It turns out there's not, I mean, consumer electronics products, not like toothbrushes,

510
00:32:15.569 --> 00:32:16.859
[S10] but electronics products.

511
00:32:16.859 --> 00:32:21.259
[S10] And so, let's assume they have one of those, right?

512
00:32:21.259 --> 00:32:21.829
[S10] Or two of those.

513
00:32:21.829 --> 00:32:25.859
[S10] Let's say they have a product that sells 2 million a year, and at a $500 ASP to them,

514
00:32:25.859 --> 00:32:30.539
[S10] so it sells to the consumer at, you know, $795 or something like that.

515
00:32:30.539 --> 00:32:33.859
[S10] That's 100, that's what, a billion dollars, right?

516
00:32:33.859 --> 00:32:36.140
[S10] 2 million a year, 500 bucks a piece.

517
00:32:36.140 --> 00:32:37.359
[S10] It's a billion dollars.

518
00:32:37.359 --> 00:32:41.319
[S10] They still have to get the other 7 billion of revenue somewhere.

519
00:32:41.319 --> 00:32:47.930
[S10] So, it's going to be an interesting transition as the ASPs get lower.

520
00:32:47.930 --> 00:32:53.349
[S10] The volumes have to get much, much higher, and you run into some scale issues when you

521
00:32:53.349 --> 00:32:55.130
[S10] look out at the consumer electronics industry.

522
00:32:55.130 --> 00:32:56.569
[S10] Not that it's not possible.

523
00:32:56.569 --> 00:32:59.009
[S10] And I think it's going to be really interesting to watch.

524
00:32:59.009 --> 00:33:06.819
[S10] And we also see a lot of, not quite that clarity of movement, but some movement in

525
00:33:06.819 --> 00:33:14.319
[S10] the Windows and PC world, again, towards taking what they have now and making it portable,

526
00:33:14.319 --> 00:33:19.289
[S10] but not giving the top third of the market more power.

527
00:33:19.289 --> 00:33:23.069
[S10] You know, System 7 on the Mac was supposed to be the second coming, and it wasn't.

528
00:33:23.069 --> 00:33:25.680
[S10] It turned out to be, you know, an incremental improvement.

529
00:33:25.680 --> 00:33:29.710
[S10] OS 2 was supposed to take us into new dimensions, and it turned out to be a complete failure.

530
00:33:29.710 --> 00:33:33.240
[S10] And they're going to throw another 100 million dollars after it, just to make sure you all

531
00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:34.240
[S10] know about it.

532
00:33:36.430 --> 00:33:43.740
[S10] And so, there's a third of those desktops out there that the users and the people that

533
00:33:43.740 --> 00:33:49.210
[S10] want to deploy more sophisticated apps are, they're in the desert, wanting something

534
00:33:49.210 --> 00:33:50.210
[S10] to drink.

535
00:33:50.210 --> 00:33:53.960
[S10] And I think there's a tremendous opportunity to give them some solutions.

536
00:33:53.960 --> 00:33:56.180
[S10] Now, that doesn't mean that Apple's not going to be successful.

537
00:33:56.180 --> 00:33:58.569
[S10] Just means they're going to go off in another direction.

538
00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:04.819
[S10] Who knows what would have happened, you know, had all this not happened?

539
00:34:05.789 --> 00:34:06.789
[S10] Yes, up in the corner.

540
00:34:08.679 --> 00:34:10.570
[S12] You've talked a lot about NeXT products.

541
00:34:10.570 --> 00:34:13.420
[S12] I wonder if you could talk about the management at NeXT?

542
00:34:13.420 --> 00:34:18.840
[S12] And if you see it changing at all as you, as you hope, go from a $120 million company

543
00:34:18.840 --> 00:34:20.650
[S12] to a $1 billion company?

544
00:34:20.650 --> 00:34:21.650
[S10] Sure.

545
00:34:24.320 --> 00:34:28.219
[S10] Yeah, we've done a lot in the last year.

546
00:34:56.429 --> 00:34:59.719
[S13] Originally met him, he was the product manager for Excel when it first came out

547
00:34:59.719 --> 00:35:01.949
[S13] and ended up running big pieces of marketing for Microsoft.

548
00:35:01.949 --> 00:35:03.389
[S13] So Mike runs all the marketing at Next.

549
00:35:03.389 --> 00:35:08.329
[S13] And Mike came in to Next just about the time when we were finally hearing what these

550
00:35:08.329 --> 00:35:11.090
[S13] big companies were saying about mission-critical custom apps.

551
00:35:11.090 --> 00:35:15.280
[S13] And so, we've worked very closely together to redefine the marketing strategy of the

552
00:35:15.280 --> 00:35:18.690
[S13] company, and Mike has done just a superb job at that.

553
00:35:18.690 --> 00:35:25.730
[S13] The second big thing we did was about three months ago, a little over three months ago,

554
00:35:25.730 --> 00:35:32.679
[S13] we consolidated our hardware design engineering, our manufacturing, our worldwide distribution,

555
00:35:32.679 --> 00:35:36.780
[S13] and hardware service, all into one chunk called the hardware division.

556
00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:40.579
[S13] So from cradle to grave, they have hardware responsibility, and we're in the process

557
00:35:40.579 --> 00:35:43.030
[S13] of forming the software division right now.

558
00:35:43.030 --> 00:35:49.920
[S13] So that we're all still under the same roof, but we have clarity about the fact that we've

559
00:35:49.920 --> 00:35:51.989
[S13] got to make two businesses successful.

560
00:35:51.989 --> 00:35:59.280
[S13] The third thing that we did, and we have a really great person running that, Rich Page,

561
00:35:59.280 --> 00:36:04.789
[S13] who's one of the founders of Next, he's doing a great job running that.

562
00:36:04.789 --> 00:36:08.449
[S13] We hired a new CFO recently, Marcel Ghani.

563
00:36:08.449 --> 00:36:13.320
[S13] He's from Intel, spent 12 years at Intel doing some pretty interesting jobs.

564
00:36:13.320 --> 00:36:17.340
[S13] He ran all of finance for Europe, he ran all of internal audit for the board, he ran

565
00:36:17.340 --> 00:36:20.780
[S13] all of manufacturing, planning and scheduling for a while, and things like that.

566
00:36:20.780 --> 00:36:25.670
[S13] He's a pretty good guy, went to Cypress for a year and a half, and then came to Next.

567
00:36:25.670 --> 00:36:27.360
[S13] And he's great.

568
00:36:27.360 --> 00:36:34.389
[S13] And then recently, as a matter of fact, about last week, I hired a chief operating officer,

569
00:36:34.389 --> 00:36:36.460
[S13] a guy named Peter Van Keilenberg.

570
00:36:36.460 --> 00:36:41.539
[S13] And Peter is someone I first met when we were looking for an executive vice president

571
00:36:41.539 --> 00:36:45.170
[S13] of sales and marketing, and he turned me down for the job, and we subsequently decided

572
00:36:45.170 --> 00:36:47.059
[S13] not to hire that position.

573
00:36:47.059 --> 00:36:50.460
[S13] And I've been chasing him for the last year and a half, almost.

574
00:36:50.460 --> 00:36:52.170
[S13] He's really good.

575
00:36:52.170 --> 00:36:59.559
[S13] He spent a lot of time at TI, has run Europe, and about three years ago went to a company

576
00:36:59.559 --> 00:37:05.579
[S13] called Mercury Communications in England, they're the MCI of the UK, and turned that

577
00:37:05.579 --> 00:37:12.579
[S13] company around to about $2 billion, and then was promoted to run half of Mercury's parent

578
00:37:12.579 --> 00:37:17.360
[S13] company, Cable and Wireless, and was running, they're about $6 billion.

579
00:37:17.360 --> 00:37:21.949
[S13] And he went there when he thought that communications and computing were going to kind of come

580
00:37:21.949 --> 00:37:24.550
[S13] together, but it never happened.

581
00:37:24.550 --> 00:37:27.699
[S13] And he figured out that his real love is in the computer business.

582
00:37:27.699 --> 00:37:29.829
[S13] He's very, very good operationally.

583
00:37:29.829 --> 00:37:32.110
[S13] So that's just happened last week.

584
00:37:32.110 --> 00:37:34.929
[S13] It seems like all the good people I really want to hire, it seems to take me a year

585
00:37:34.929 --> 00:37:36.449
[S13] to hire them.

586
00:37:36.449 --> 00:37:38.329
[S13] And it's always been that way, even at Apple.

587
00:37:38.329 --> 00:37:42.260
[S13] Some of the best technical people, or whoever, it always seemed to take me like a year to

588
00:37:42.260 --> 00:37:44.449
[S13] pry them out of HP, or wherever.

589
00:37:44.449 --> 00:37:48.260
[S13] And it took me over a year to hire them.

590
00:37:48.260 --> 00:37:52.789
[S13] I think Mike has the award, you're about a year and a half.

591
00:37:52.789 --> 00:37:54.789
[S13] And they're all worth it.

592
00:37:54.789 --> 00:37:59.289
[S13] What happens is I usually meet somebody that is really good, I think is very, very good,

593
00:37:59.289 --> 00:38:03.980
[S13] and you can't get them, and then you go try to find other people, and nobody measures

594
00:38:03.980 --> 00:38:06.980
[S13] up, you know, when you meet somebody that good, you always compare them to this one

595
00:38:06.980 --> 00:38:11.539
[S13] person, and you know you're going to be settling for second best if you compromise.

596
00:38:11.539 --> 00:38:19.449
[S13] I've always found it best not to compromise and just keep chipping away.

597
00:38:19.449 --> 00:38:22.070
[S13] So I think we're doing quite a bit.

598
00:38:22.070 --> 00:38:25.449
[S13] It feels like we're running a company a lot better now than we ever have.

599
00:38:25.449 --> 00:38:27.760
[S13] We've definitely made our share of mistakes.

600
00:38:27.760 --> 00:38:28.760
[S13] Yeah?

601
00:38:28.760 --> 00:38:34.980
[S14] What technological advances do you see coming over the next five to 10 years?

602
00:38:34.980 --> 00:38:39.619
[S14] And how is Next's structure taking advantage of that?

603
00:38:39.619 --> 00:38:47.449
[S13] I'll give you a global answer, then we can descend into some details.

604
00:38:47.449 --> 00:38:55.010
[S13] I believe that you can use the concept of technology windows opening, you know, and

605
00:38:55.010 --> 00:38:57.949
[S13] then eventually closing.

606
00:38:57.949 --> 00:39:05.199
[S13] And what I mean by that is enough technology, usually from fairly diverse places, comes

607
00:39:05.199 --> 00:39:11.119
[S13] together and makes something that's a quantum leap forward possible.

608
00:39:11.119 --> 00:39:12.360
[S13] And it doesn't come out of nowhere.

609
00:39:12.360 --> 00:39:16.280
[S13] If you poke around the labs, and you hang around, you know, the media lab here at MIT

610
00:39:16.280 --> 00:39:20.860
[S13] and other places, you can kind of get a feel for some of those things.

611
00:39:20.860 --> 00:39:24.889
[S13] And usually, they're not quite possible, but all of a sudden, you start to sense things

612
00:39:24.889 --> 00:39:29.519
[S13] coming together, and the planets lining up to where this is now possible, or barely

613
00:39:29.519 --> 00:39:30.829
[S13] possible.

614
00:39:30.829 --> 00:39:33.469
[S13] And a window opens up.

615
00:39:33.469 --> 00:39:38.269
[S13] And it usually takes around, my experience anyway, my life has been, it takes around

616
00:39:38.269 --> 00:39:47.769
[S13] five years to create a commercial product that takes advantage of that technical window

617
00:39:47.769 --> 00:39:48.769
[S13] opening up.

618
00:39:48.769 --> 00:39:52.019
[S13] Sometimes you start before the window's quite open, and you can't get through it,

619
00:39:52.019 --> 00:39:53.659
[S13] and you push it up, and you push it up.

620
00:39:53.659 --> 00:39:55.219
[S13] Sometimes it just takes a lot of work.

621
00:39:55.590 --> 00:39:58.019
[S15] Took that long, you know, with the Apple II.

622
00:39:58.050 --> 00:39:59.659
[S15] Took that long with the Mac.

623
00:39:59.690 --> 00:40:02.260
[S15] You know, took a Lisa along the way.

624
00:40:02.289 --> 00:40:03.920
[S15] Hundred million dollars.

625
00:40:03.949 --> 00:40:04.710
[S15] It takes a while.

626
00:40:04.739 --> 00:40:06.739
[S15] It's a lot expensive to push those windows open.

627
00:40:08.440 --> 00:40:11.840
[S15] And in our case, you know, our first product failed.

628
00:40:11.869 --> 00:40:15.239
[S15] We came out with this Cube and we sold 10,000 of them.

629
00:40:15.269 --> 00:40:15.949
[S15] Why?

630
00:40:15.980 --> 00:40:19.980
[S15] Because we weren't quite there yet and we made some mistakes along the way and we had

631
00:40:20.010 --> 00:40:21.210
[S15] to course correct.

632
00:40:21.239 --> 00:40:24.179
[S15] You know, Macintosh was a course correction off the Lisa.

633
00:40:24.210 --> 00:40:27.869
[S15] So with Apple II and III, we did it in reverse.

634
00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:37.760
[S15] It takes around five years or some number of years like that to realize that window

635
00:40:37.789 --> 00:40:38.619
[S15] opening.

636
00:40:38.650 --> 00:40:43.769
[S15] And then it seems to take about another five years to really exploit it in the marketplace.

637
00:40:43.800 --> 00:40:46.710
[S15] And let me give you some examples from my life.

638
00:40:46.739 --> 00:40:51.539
[S15] Apple II lasted 15 years.

639
00:40:51.570 --> 00:40:52.780
[S15] 15 years.

640
00:40:52.809 --> 00:40:55.809
[S15] The hardware churned, but basically it was the same for 15 years.

641
00:40:55.840 --> 00:40:59.559
[S15] DOS, you know, DOS just passed 10 years.

642
00:40:59.590 --> 00:41:04.639
[S15] I don't think anyone would disagree that it's going to easily last another five, right?

643
00:41:04.670 --> 00:41:05.559
[S15] Unfortunately.

644
00:41:07.420 --> 00:41:11.340
[S15] And Mac, you know, Mac is eight years old, right?

645
00:41:11.369 --> 00:41:14.500
[S15] No question it's going to last another four or five years, right?

646
00:41:14.530 --> 00:41:15.639
[S15] These things are hard.

647
00:41:15.670 --> 00:41:20.159
[S15] It's not, they don't last because it's convenient or even because it's economic.

648
00:41:20.190 --> 00:41:23.989
[S15] They last because they're really, this is hard stuff to do.

649
00:41:24.019 --> 00:41:31.940
[S15] And so when we are pushing that window open, I think with our current generation of products,

650
00:41:31.969 --> 00:41:33.510
[S15] we finally got the window open.

651
00:41:33.539 --> 00:41:35.110
[S15] After six years, it's open.

652
00:41:35.139 --> 00:41:38.010
[S15] We've got an extremely elegant implementation.

653
00:41:38.039 --> 00:41:42.059
[S15] And we've got five years of work to do to exploit it in the marketplace.

654
00:41:42.090 --> 00:41:44.460
[S15] And when, you know, we'll peak in five years.

655
00:41:44.489 --> 00:41:47.219
[S15] Five years, we'll all sit around and say, okay, it's time to get started on the next

656
00:41:47.250 --> 00:41:48.780
[S15] thing.

657
00:41:48.809 --> 00:41:51.599
[S15] It's time to get going on the next thing, maybe four years from now.

658
00:41:51.630 --> 00:41:56.420
[S15] But we've got a lot of work ahead of us just to move this thing out and educate the market

659
00:41:56.449 --> 00:41:59.400
[S15] and continue to refine it based on market feedback.

660
00:41:59.429 --> 00:42:06.599
[S15] So everything I know about technology windows that are open or just about open is in next

661
00:42:06.630 --> 00:42:09.239
[S15] step or we're working on it in the labs.

662
00:42:09.269 --> 00:42:11.579
[S15] And these things generally don't come along independently.

663
00:42:11.610 --> 00:42:15.079
[S15] They kind of clumps of them come together, has been my experience.

664
00:42:15.110 --> 00:42:22.019
[S15] So the things that aren't in there right now that I can talk about, there's some video

665
00:42:22.050 --> 00:42:24.610
[S15] stuff that's really interesting that's going to be integrated in.

666
00:42:24.639 --> 00:42:28.550
[S15] There's some security stuff that's really fascinating that's integrating in.

667
00:42:28.579 --> 00:42:33.800
[S15] But most of the core technologies in there, products are getting smaller and portable,

668
00:42:33.829 --> 00:42:37.550
[S15] products are getting much, much faster, but these things are well-known.

669
00:42:37.579 --> 00:42:42.110
[S15] You know, the products that we can put on, that we can give to you in the next, in the

670
00:42:42.139 --> 00:42:47.579
[S15] next year or two are going to be running at speeds that I find hard to believe.

671
00:42:47.610 --> 00:42:49.840
[S15] And I've been doing this for a while.

672
00:42:49.869 --> 00:42:57.699
[S15] So I think the window's open and I think object-oriented technology is the biggest

673
00:42:57.730 --> 00:43:04.590
[S15] technical breakthrough I have seen since the early 80s with graphical user interfaces.

674
00:43:04.619 --> 00:43:06.110
[S15] And I think it's bigger, actually.

675
00:43:06.139 --> 00:43:08.300
[S15] It will prove to be bigger over time.

676
00:43:08.329 --> 00:43:09.139
[S15] Yes?

677
00:43:09.510 --> 00:43:10.340
[S16] I have a question.

678
00:43:10.369 --> 00:43:16.079
[S16] If the value of the diagram is from things like operational applications and next step

679
00:43:16.110 --> 00:43:20.320
[S16] is a tool to help you build those, where do you draw the line in capturing the most

680
00:43:20.349 --> 00:43:24.639
[S16] value between actually developing the application, something like Word or Excel, and becoming

681
00:43:24.670 --> 00:43:28.840
[S16] a tool builder to support those people who may capture a little bit more of the value?

682
00:43:28.869 --> 00:43:30.840
[S16] Where do you want to draw the line there?

683
00:43:31.909 --> 00:43:32.940
[S15] If you're a software company?

684
00:43:32.969 --> 00:43:34.179
[S16] If you're a software company.

685
00:43:34.210 --> 00:43:36.949
[S15] Well, Microsoft's made it easy.

686
00:43:36.980 --> 00:43:37.750
[S16] Repeat the question.

687
00:43:37.780 --> 00:43:38.250
[S15] Yeah, I'm sorry.

688
00:43:38.280 --> 00:43:41.820
[S15] The question is, if you're a software company, should you develop apps or should you develop

689
00:43:41.849 --> 00:43:43.429
[S15] objects and tools?

690
00:43:43.460 --> 00:43:47.369
[S15] And see, the software industry with Microsoft and Lotus and WordPerfect's made it real

691
00:43:47.400 --> 00:43:48.099
[S15] easy.

692
00:43:48.130 --> 00:43:51.869
[S15] Unless you've got 20 or 30 million dollars burning a hole in your pocket, so you can

693
00:43:51.900 --> 00:43:56.139
[S15] go out and hire a few hundred people to develop what it takes to develop one of these polished,

694
00:43:56.170 --> 00:44:02.449
[S15] shrink-wrapped apps on Mac or on Windows, then you don't have much of a choice.

695
00:44:02.480 --> 00:44:07.179
[S15] There's not a real opportunity there to, assuming you had the best spreadsheet today,

696
00:44:07.210 --> 00:44:12.139
[S15] you could easily spend 50 million dollars marketing it before you'd be breaking even

697
00:44:12.159 --> 00:44:17.219
[S15] selling it because of just how expensive it is to market a product today.

698
00:44:17.349 --> 00:44:20.949
[S16] But that's only for existing spreadsheets if you're trying to develop a next level

699
00:44:20.969 --> 00:44:23.280
[S16] generation of different type of products.

700
00:44:23.309 --> 00:44:27.739
[S15] Well, assume that you have a breakthrough spreadsheet.

701
00:44:57.139 --> 00:45:01.739
[S17] You've mentioned that a possible problem for NeXT is the lack of developers and consultants

702
00:45:01.739 --> 00:45:03.320
[S17] out there to recommend it.

703
00:45:03.320 --> 00:45:09.809
[S17] Based on that view, what's the next view of how to market itself to academic institutions?

704
00:45:09.809 --> 00:45:17.400
[S18] I think your question is, what is our philosophy on marketing our products to higher

705
00:45:17.400 --> 00:45:18.400
[S18] education?

706
00:45:18.400 --> 00:45:25.170
[S18] Well, we started off selling only to higher education, which arguably was a mistake.

707
00:45:25.170 --> 00:45:28.389
[S18] And, but we've done really well there and our hearts are there.

708
00:45:28.389 --> 00:45:32.030
[S18] And we sold a lot of Macintoshes there, too, when we were at Apple.

709
00:45:32.030 --> 00:45:34.460
[S18] And I think it's a no-brainer.

710
00:45:34.460 --> 00:45:38.130
[S18] I think you take your products, you discount them as heavily as you can, you sell them

711
00:45:38.130 --> 00:45:39.769
[S18] into higher education.

712
00:45:39.769 --> 00:45:43.500
[S18] Higher education's a wonderful place to give you great feedback about how to make your

713
00:45:43.500 --> 00:45:45.880
[S18] products better and what's wrong with them.

714
00:45:45.880 --> 00:45:52.090
[S18] And it's a great place to educate bright people that you can hire and that your customers

715
00:45:52.090 --> 00:45:53.519
[S18] can hire when they graduate.

716
00:45:53.519 --> 00:45:54.610
[S18] So we do exactly that.

717
00:45:54.610 --> 00:45:59.429
[S18] We have, I think we're sold on about 350 campuses in the United States.

718
00:45:59.429 --> 00:46:03.300
[S18] We are clearly the number one selling workstation across the US.

719
00:46:03.300 --> 00:46:09.239
[S18] And we're the number two selling computer of any type at campuses like here MIT or Stanford,

720
00:46:09.239 --> 00:46:10.519
[S18] right behind the Macintosh.

721
00:46:10.519 --> 00:46:19.409
[S18] We sell more computers at MIT than PCs through the institutional resale engines.

722
00:46:19.409 --> 00:46:23.809
[S18] So, we do as much as we can, and I think it's been pretty effective.

723
00:46:23.809 --> 00:46:24.809
[S18] What do you think?

724
00:46:24.809 --> 00:46:27.409
[S17] I mean, for example, Project Athena is going away.

725
00:46:27.409 --> 00:46:29.809
[S17] Like, the support for IBM and Dell is going.

726
00:46:29.809 --> 00:46:32.769
[S17] I mean, I'm talking about not just selling computers, I'm talking about establishing

727
00:46:32.769 --> 00:46:37.409
[S17] a network of an institutionalized system of NeXT.

728
00:46:37.409 --> 00:46:44.010
[S18] Well, what's happening is, the Project Athena didn't succeed for a reason.

729
00:46:44.010 --> 00:46:49.849
[S18] They didn't succeed because they had a lot of good ideas as a research project, but the

730
00:46:49.849 --> 00:46:55.769
[S18] people necessary to commercialize those things and make them into real products aren't necessarily

731
00:46:55.769 --> 00:47:00.670
[S18] the same people that are going to pioneer the ideas at a university like MIT.

732
00:47:00.670 --> 00:47:04.800
[S18] And so, those projects never quite get baked.

733
00:47:04.800 --> 00:47:09.000
[S18] The recipe's kind of developed and you make a few samples and it's pretty good, but the

734
00:47:09.000 --> 00:47:12.710
[S18] computer industry is pretty advanced, and so other people pick up those ideas and make

735
00:47:12.710 --> 00:47:16.030
[S18] them into real products, and they kind of leave the research projects in the dust if

736
00:47:16.030 --> 00:47:20.110
[S18] the researchers drop research and start trying to commercialize stuff.

737
00:47:20.110 --> 00:47:23.550
[S18] And I can point to 100 examples of that in higher education.

738
00:47:23.550 --> 00:47:28.400
[S18] So, it's probably good that Project Athena has a beginning, a middle, and an end, so

739
00:47:28.400 --> 00:47:32.960
[S18] that those people don't get stuck trying to do commercial software in an academic environment.

740
00:47:32.960 --> 00:47:35.960
[S18] It's kind of a mismatch.

741
00:47:35.960 --> 00:47:40.300
[S18] As far as we're concerned, what we're doing is, there's a lot of labs that are being put

742
00:47:40.300 --> 00:47:43.829
[S18] in, in higher education, and we're winning almost every one of those.

743
00:47:43.829 --> 00:47:47.579
[S18] And we really go hard after the labs so that people that can't afford the computers have

744
00:47:47.579 --> 00:47:49.110
[S18] public access.

745
00:47:49.110 --> 00:47:53.469
[S18] And most universities now have fairly elaborate campus-wide networks.

746
00:47:53.469 --> 00:47:57.110
[S18] It's no longer a new, cutting-edge thing, and we plug right into those.

747
00:47:57.110 --> 00:48:02.139
[S18] So, I think it's, life's moved beyond where it was a few years ago, where those kind of

748
00:48:02.139 --> 00:48:04.300
[S18] projects were really important.

749
00:48:04.300 --> 00:48:07.000
[S18] And the knowledge to do that exists fairly widespread.

750
00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:09.329
[S18] A few more in, yeah?

751
00:48:09.329 --> 00:48:13.610
[S17] What's the most important thing that you personally learned at Apple that you're

752
00:48:13.610 --> 00:48:15.110
[S17] doing at NeXT?

753
00:48:15.110 --> 00:48:17.829
[S18] Good question.

754
00:48:37.170 --> 00:48:43.030
[S18] I'm not sure I learned this when I was at Apple, but I learned it based on the data

755
00:48:43.030 --> 00:48:45.469
[S18] when I was at Apple.

756
00:48:45.469 --> 00:48:54.219
[S18] And that is, I now take a longer-term view on people.

757
00:48:54.219 --> 00:49:00.250
[S18] In other words, when I see something not being done right, my first reaction isn't

758
00:49:00.250 --> 00:49:03.860
[S18] to go fix it.

759
00:49:03.860 --> 00:49:10.170
[S18] It's to say, we're building a team here and we're going to do great stuff for the next

760
00:49:10.170 --> 00:49:12.500
[S18] decade, not just the next year.

761
00:49:12.500 --> 00:49:20.650
[S18] And so, what do I need to do to help so that the person that's screwing up learns, versus

762
00:49:20.650 --> 00:49:23.110
[S18] how do I fix the problem?

763
00:49:23.110 --> 00:49:26.550
[S18] And that's painful sometimes.

764
00:49:26.550 --> 00:49:32.820
[S18] And I still have that first instinct to go fix the problem, but that's, taking a longer-term

765
00:49:32.820 --> 00:49:35.610
[S18] view on people is probably the biggest thing that's changed.

766
00:49:35.610 --> 00:49:42.320
[S18] And I don't know, that's, you know, maybe the part of that's biological, but, yes.

767
00:49:42.320 --> 00:49:47.260
[S19] To follow up on that question, I wanted to ask you about the management style, specifically

768
00:49:47.260 --> 00:49:50.110
[S19] how do you resolve conflict during the conversation?

769
00:49:50.110 --> 00:49:51.960
[S18] Management, what's our management style?

770
00:49:51.960 --> 00:49:53.110
[S18] How do we resolve conflict?

771
00:49:54.769 --> 00:49:59.269
[S20] I've never believed in the theory that if we're on the same management team

772
00:49:59.269 --> 00:50:03.840
[S20] and a decision has to be made and I decide in a way that you don't like,

773
00:50:03.840 --> 00:50:06.909
[S20] and I say, "Come on, buy into the decision."

774
00:50:06.909 --> 00:50:08.130
[S20] You know, buy into it.

775
00:50:08.130 --> 00:50:09.219
[S20] We're all on the same team.

776
00:50:09.219 --> 00:50:10.829
[S20] You don't agree, but buy into it.

777
00:50:10.829 --> 00:50:12.380
[S20] Let's go make it happen.

778
00:50:12.380 --> 00:50:16.510
[S20] Because what happens is sooner or later,

779
00:50:16.510 --> 00:50:20.579
[S20] you're paying somebody to do what they think is right.

780
00:50:20.579 --> 00:50:24.199
[S20] But then you're trying to get them to do what they think isn't right.

781
00:50:24.199 --> 00:50:28.550
[S20] And sooner or later, it outs and you end up having that conflict.

782
00:50:28.550 --> 00:50:34.219
[S20] So I've always felt that the best way is to get everybody in a room

783
00:50:34.219 --> 00:50:37.659
[S20] and talk it through until you agree.

784
00:50:37.659 --> 00:50:40.659
[S20] Now, that's not everybody in the company.

785
00:50:40.659 --> 00:50:45.909
[S20] But that's everybody that's really involved in that decision

786
00:50:45.909 --> 00:50:48.449
[S20] that needs to execute it.

787
00:50:48.449 --> 00:50:53.019
[S20] And so that's how we try to run Next.

788
00:50:53.019 --> 00:50:55.230
[S20] The way we run Next is we have a team at the top.

789
00:50:55.230 --> 00:50:56.289
[S20] We call the policy team.

790
00:50:56.289 --> 00:50:59.260
[S20] There's eight people.

791
00:50:59.260 --> 00:51:00.070
[S20] Mike is on it.

792
00:51:00.070 --> 00:51:00.570
[S20] I'm on it.

793
00:51:00.570 --> 00:51:02.019
[S20] We have six other people on it.

794
00:51:02.019 --> 00:51:05.070
[S20] And the key, we have two things we try to do.

795
00:51:05.070 --> 00:51:09.099
[S20] One is, we try to differentiate between the really important decisions

796
00:51:09.099 --> 00:51:11.260
[S20] and the ones that we don't have to make.

797
00:51:11.260 --> 00:51:15.900
[S20] And the really important ones, we work on it till we all agree.

798
00:51:15.900 --> 00:51:20.150
[S20] Because we're paying people to tell us what to do.

799
00:51:20.150 --> 00:51:23.010
[S20] In other words, I don't view that we pay people to do things.

800
00:51:23.010 --> 00:51:24.900
[S20] That's easy, to find people to do things.

801
00:51:24.900 --> 00:51:28.329
[S20] What's harder is to find people to tell you what should be done.

802
00:51:28.329 --> 00:51:28.860
[S20] Right?

803
00:51:28.860 --> 00:51:29.739
[S20] That's what we look for.

804
00:51:29.739 --> 00:51:31.960
[S20] So we pay people a lot of money.

805
00:51:31.960 --> 00:51:34.190
[S20] And we expect them to tell us what to do.

806
00:51:34.190 --> 00:51:37.940
[S20] And so when that's your attitude,

807
00:51:37.940 --> 00:51:41.260
[S20] you shouldn't run off and do things if people don't all feel good about them.

808
00:51:41.260 --> 00:51:43.989
[S20] And the key to making that work is to realize

809
00:51:43.989 --> 00:51:48.039
[S20] there's not that many things that any one team really has to decide.

810
00:51:48.039 --> 00:51:52.070
[S20] And we might have 25 really important things

811
00:51:52.070 --> 00:51:54.400
[S20] we have to decide on a year.

812
00:51:54.400 --> 00:51:56.820
[S20] Not a lot.

813
00:51:56.820 --> 00:51:59.400
[S20] So that's how we try to run it.

814
00:51:59.400 --> 00:52:03.840
[S20] Sometimes it works and sometimes we're still working on it.

815
00:52:03.840 --> 00:52:08.030
[S20] I can't think of once, I can't, maybe there's once or twice,

816
00:52:08.030 --> 00:52:10.460
[S20] but I can't even recall a time when I've said,

817
00:52:10.460 --> 00:52:13.619
[S20] "Damn it, I'm the CEO and we're doing it this way."

818
00:52:13.619 --> 00:52:15.780
[S20] You know?

819
00:52:15.780 --> 00:52:17.369
[S20] I can recall a time when I've said,

820
00:52:17.369 --> 00:52:20.840
[S20] "We don't see eye to eye," and you're off the team.

821
00:52:25.739 --> 00:52:29.530
[S20] I've had to say that once or twice over a prolonged period of time

822
00:52:29.530 --> 00:52:34.309
[S20] when a person has not wanted to go in the same direction

823
00:52:34.309 --> 00:52:35.960
[S20] we've wanted to go in as a team.

824
00:52:35.960 --> 00:52:37.780
[S20] It's my job every once in a while to say,

825
00:52:37.780 --> 00:52:39.409
[S20] "Hey, you want to go this way?

826
00:52:39.409 --> 00:52:41.019
[S20] We want to go this way."

827
00:52:41.019 --> 00:52:42.690
[S20] It's not working.

828
00:52:42.690 --> 00:52:49.130
[S20] So, but when people are on the team, then we work it out.

829
00:52:51.300 --> 00:52:51.800
[S20] Yeah?

830
00:52:52.159 --> 00:52:54.489
[S21] You've kind of chosen this direction, this niche

831
00:52:54.489 --> 00:52:57.130
[S21] towards the premium product.

832
00:52:57.130 --> 00:53:00.210
[S21] Versus you've discussed Macintosh, going into portability

833
00:53:00.210 --> 00:53:01.909
[S21] and a lot of this portability issue.

834
00:53:01.909 --> 00:53:05.019
[S21] Do you think portability is exclusive?

835
00:53:05.019 --> 00:53:09.800
[S21] Can you do that and still do some of the market-driven type of stuff as well?

836
00:53:09.800 --> 00:53:12.960
[S20] I want to come back to your premium product characterization

837
00:53:12.960 --> 00:53:15.130
[S20] because our products actually cost, you know,

838
00:53:15.130 --> 00:53:17.130
[S20] close to half of what Sun's do.

839
00:53:17.130 --> 00:53:20.800
[S20] And the reason they do is we have the most automated factory in the industry.

840
00:53:20.800 --> 00:53:24.329
[S20] And we have a great VLSI design group which designs stuff in a lot less part.

841
00:53:24.329 --> 00:53:26.329
[S20] Do you guys, manufacturing, do you care about that?

842
00:53:26.329 --> 00:53:28.070
[S22] Yes.

843
00:53:28.070 --> 00:53:31.510
[S20] We should talk about that in a minute.

844
00:53:31.510 --> 00:53:34.269
[S20] The industry is bifurcating right now.

845
00:53:34.269 --> 00:53:36.769
[S20] And what's happening is, is that the Macs and the PCs

846
00:53:36.769 --> 00:53:38.739
[S20] as you know them today are all going to be just like this.

847
00:53:38.739 --> 00:53:39.880
[S20] Hold up your PowerBook.

848
00:53:39.880 --> 00:53:44.880
[S20] All going to be just like this, only lighter and smaller before very long.

849
00:53:44.880 --> 00:53:48.099
[S20] And they're taking the technology we have today,

850
00:53:48.099 --> 00:53:49.349
[S20] not particularly changing it,

851
00:53:49.349 --> 00:53:53.489
[S20] and getting it off the desktop in portable forms.

852
00:53:53.489 --> 00:53:55.190
[S20] And they're giving up a few things for that,

853
00:54:43.369 --> 00:54:46.719
[S23] You're not going to do that with a radio LAN any time in the next five years.

854
00:54:46.719 --> 00:54:50.480
[S23] Another thing our customers want is our mix has shifted to like 80% color.

855
00:54:50.480 --> 00:54:52.489
[S23] And they want true color to do photographs.

856
00:54:52.489 --> 00:54:57.070
[S23] There is no flat panel color display today that will do photographic quality color.

857
00:54:57.070 --> 00:55:00.590
[S23] And they want big ones because once you have multitasking and you can run a bunch of

858
00:55:00.590 --> 00:55:03.949
[S23] things, once you need a bigger window into this electronic world.

859
00:55:03.949 --> 00:55:07.760
[S23] When you're using your computer for two or three hours a day, you don't want to be looking,

860
00:55:07.760 --> 00:55:09.159
[S23] you know, through blinders this big.

861
00:55:09.159 --> 00:55:11.090
[S23] You want something bigger.

862
00:55:11.090 --> 00:55:15.150
[S23] So these are all the enemy of that today.

863
00:55:15.150 --> 00:55:21.260
[S23] And we're working on smaller products for sure, but it's really tough to get both.

864
00:55:21.260 --> 00:55:25.230
[S23] And we're optimizing for the power.

865
00:55:25.230 --> 00:55:30.429
[S23] Because we see a giant hole there for running these mission-critical custom apps.

866
00:55:30.429 --> 00:55:33.550
[S23] And what people are doing needs more power.

867
00:55:33.550 --> 00:55:35.480
[S23] So that's what we're optimizing for.

868
00:55:35.480 --> 00:55:36.800
[S23] But let's talk about manufacturing.

869
00:55:36.800 --> 00:55:38.730
[S23] How many of you have a manufacturing background?

870
00:55:38.730 --> 00:55:40.570
[S23] Oh, that's great.

871
00:55:40.570 --> 00:55:42.119
[S23] I love manufacturing.

872
00:55:42.119 --> 00:55:43.550
[S23] And what kinds of things?

873
00:55:43.550 --> 00:55:45.619
[S23] What kinds of companies?

874
00:55:45.619 --> 00:55:48.090
[S24] Pharmaceuticals.

875
00:55:48.090 --> 00:55:49.469
[S23] Pharmaceuticals?

876
00:55:49.469 --> 00:55:51.219
[S23] Any auto people here?

877
00:55:51.219 --> 00:55:52.219
[S25] Auto.

878
00:55:52.219 --> 00:55:52.530
[S23] Auto?

879
00:55:52.530 --> 00:55:53.530
[S23] Electronics?

880
00:55:53.530 --> 00:55:54.530
[S23] Which?

881
00:55:54.530 --> 00:55:55.530
[S23] TI?

882
00:55:55.530 --> 00:55:56.530
[S26] Motorola.

883
00:55:56.530 --> 00:55:57.530
[S23] Motorola?

884
00:55:57.530 --> 00:55:59.559
[S27] Is it true you're saying you're manufacturing overseas?

885
00:55:59.559 --> 00:56:00.559
[S27] Or is this a...

886
00:56:00.559 --> 00:56:01.949
[S23] I heard that rumor too.

887
00:56:01.949 --> 00:56:04.559
[S23] No, it couldn't be further from the truth.

888
00:56:04.559 --> 00:56:07.139
[S23] We love manufacturing at NEXT.

889
00:56:07.139 --> 00:56:12.329
[S23] And when I was at Apple, I had the good fortune to lead the effort to build the Mac

890
00:56:12.329 --> 00:56:13.329
[S23] factory.

891
00:56:13.329 --> 00:56:16.250
[S23] And we designed and built and operated that factory.

892
00:56:16.250 --> 00:56:18.179
[S23] And it was a real breakthrough.

893
00:56:18.179 --> 00:56:21.179
[S23] It was the best factory in the industry till we built the one at NEXT.

894
00:56:21.179 --> 00:56:22.650
[S23] And we made a lot of...

895
00:56:22.650 --> 00:56:26.099
[S23] We made a lot of mistakes, though.

896
00:56:26.099 --> 00:56:28.289
[S23] As an example, I remember walking through it.

897
00:56:28.289 --> 00:56:31.860
[S23] You know, one of the things you learn when you start building factories is that warehouses

898
00:56:31.860 --> 00:56:33.219
[S23] are really bad.

899
00:56:33.219 --> 00:56:33.679
[S23] Right?

900
00:56:33.679 --> 00:56:36.469
[S23] Warehouses are bad because you tend to put things in them.

901
00:56:36.469 --> 00:56:38.429
[S23] And inventory is really bad.

902
00:56:38.429 --> 00:56:43.429
[S23] Inventory is really bad because if it's defective, you don't find out about it for a while, and

903
00:56:43.429 --> 00:56:47.000
[S23] you don't close the quality feedback loop with the vendor and correct the problem till

904
00:56:47.000 --> 00:56:48.400
[S23] they've made a zillion of them.

905
00:56:48.400 --> 00:56:51.769
[S23] What you want to do is find the problem, the first one that comes in the door, and stop

906
00:56:51.769 --> 00:56:53.639
[S23] them from making more until you fix the problem.

907
00:56:53.639 --> 00:56:57.579
[S23] So warehouses also cost money because you put all this stuff in them, and the stuff

908
00:56:57.579 --> 00:57:00.869
[S23] you have to go borrow money from the bank or use money that could be, you know, used

909
00:57:00.869 --> 00:57:01.869
[S23] in a more productive purpose.

910
00:57:01.869 --> 00:57:03.980
[S23] So warehouses are bad.

911
00:57:03.980 --> 00:57:05.949
[S23] And you want to go to JIT.

912
00:57:05.949 --> 00:57:09.329
[S23] I'm sure you've studied this all and studied examples.

913
00:57:09.329 --> 00:57:12.650
[S23] I was walking through the Mac factory one day, and the two biggest pieces of automation

914
00:57:12.650 --> 00:57:17.420
[S23] we put in were a giant small parts storage and retrieval system.

915
00:57:17.420 --> 00:57:19.480
[S23] It was these totes that ran around.

916
00:57:19.480 --> 00:57:23.420
[S23] And the second one was this giant burn-in system at the end.

917
00:57:23.420 --> 00:57:26.869
[S23] And, you know, a few tens of millions of dollars' worth of equipment.

918
00:57:26.869 --> 00:57:32.840
[S23] And I realized, unfortunately too late, that both of them are warehouses.

919
00:57:32.840 --> 00:57:34.230
[S23] They're just high-tech warehouses.

920
00:57:34.230 --> 00:57:36.340
[S23] Right?

921
00:57:36.340 --> 00:57:43.510
[S23] And so when we looked at NEXT, we said, no warehouses of any kind.

922
00:57:43.510 --> 00:57:45.760
[S23] We have a true JIT factory.

923
00:57:45.760 --> 00:57:49.449
[S23] Stuff comes in and is delivered right to the point of use on the factory floor.

924
00:57:49.449 --> 00:57:51.760
[S23] There is no warehouse.

925
00:57:51.760 --> 00:57:55.760
[S23] Deliveries are made daily, sometimes more frequently than that.

926
00:57:55.760 --> 00:57:58.840
[S23] There is no outgoing warehouse.

927
00:57:58.840 --> 00:58:01.170
[S23] Everything is visible.

928
00:58:01.170 --> 00:58:10.369
[S23] And the reason that we were able to do a lot of what we've done is because we looked

929
00:58:10.369 --> 00:58:11.369
[S23] at manu...

930
00:58:11.369 --> 00:58:12.559
[S23] Well, let me give you an example.

931
00:58:12.559 --> 00:58:16.420
[S23] When we were learning about manufacturing at Mac, we hired a Stanford Business School

932
00:58:16.420 --> 00:58:20.730
[S23] professor at the time named Stephen Wheelwright, who Harvard's since stolen away, I think.

933
00:58:20.730 --> 00:58:21.900
[S23] And he did a neat thing.

934
00:58:21.900 --> 00:58:23.619
[S23] He drew on the board a little chart.

935
00:58:23.619 --> 00:58:29.039
[S23] First time I met him, he said, "You can view all companies from a manufacturing perspective

936
00:58:29.039 --> 00:58:30.039
[S23] this way."

937
00:58:30.039 --> 00:58:32.119
[S23] You can say, there's five stages.

938
00:58:32.119 --> 00:58:33.619
[S23] One, two, three, four, five.

939
00:58:33.619 --> 00:58:35.340
[S23] Right there, all have these things.

940
00:58:35.340 --> 00:58:41.059
[S23] Stage one is companies that view manufacturing as a necessary evil.

941
00:59:43.690 --> 00:59:48.570
[S28] I put Compaq in that category and IBM's maybe stage two and things like that.

942
00:59:48.570 --> 00:59:52.579
[S28] And I think there's only, there's only, you know, Sun's maybe sort of in the middle, maybe

943
00:59:52.579 --> 00:59:53.159
[S28] here.

944
00:59:53.159 --> 00:59:58.619
[S28] I think there's only three companies here, and that's us, Apple, and Microsoft in stage

945
00:59:58.619 --> 00:59:59.119
[S28] five.

946
00:59:59.119 --> 01:00:01.300
[S28] We start everything with the software and work back.

947
01:00:01.300 --> 01:00:08.429
[S28] But anyway, going back to manufacturing, we started looking at the factory as a software

948
01:00:08.429 --> 01:00:10.539
[S28] problem.

949
01:00:10.539 --> 01:00:13.809
[S28] And the first people we hired in the factory were some software engineers.

950
01:00:13.809 --> 01:00:17.269
[S28] We convinced them to move from R and D into software, which was not easy.

951
01:00:17.269 --> 01:00:20.769
[S28] We had to give them bonuses, we had to cajole them, we had to promise them they could come

952
01:00:20.769 --> 01:00:22.269
[S28] back if they hated it.

953
01:00:22.269 --> 01:00:26.320
[S28] And they went over there and we said, "This is really just a software problem with interesting

954
01:00:26.320 --> 01:00:28.070
[S28] I/O devices called robots."

955
01:00:28.070 --> 01:00:29.300
[S28] That's all it is.

956
01:00:29.300 --> 01:00:31.840
[S28] And so we started building the software first.

957
01:00:31.840 --> 01:00:39.980
[S28] And our first robots that we got, we specced them out and we bought them completely turnkey

958
01:00:39.980 --> 01:00:45.590
[S28] with the robot arms on them and all the electronics and the software to control them.

959
01:00:45.590 --> 01:00:48.590
[S28] And we specced it out, but we didn't write it.

960
01:00:48.590 --> 01:00:50.170
[S28] And they didn't, they worked okay.

961
01:00:50.170 --> 01:00:53.150
[S28] Some of them are still in use, but they weren't great.

962
01:00:53.150 --> 01:00:57.369
[S28] And being software folks, we weren't real happy.

963
01:00:57.369 --> 01:00:58.059
[S28] They weren't elegant.

964
01:00:58.059 --> 01:00:59.670
[S28] We couldn't do what we wanted with the robots.

965
01:00:59.670 --> 01:01:02.840
[S28] We couldn't tie in a quality information system to them and all this other stuff we

966
01:01:02.840 --> 01:01:03.090
[S28] wanted.

967
01:01:03.090 --> 01:01:07.369
[S28] So the second generation, we specced out the hardware and had somebody build the hardware

968
01:01:07.369 --> 01:01:11.130
[S28] for us, but we wrote all the software on our own computers.

969
01:01:11.130 --> 01:01:15.519
[S28] We're object oriented, so we started writing robot objects, quality objects, you know,

970
01:01:15.519 --> 01:01:18.059
[S28] all sorts of objects to control this factory.

971
01:01:18.059 --> 01:01:20.869
[S28] And we found out our computer was great for it.

972
01:01:20.869 --> 01:01:26.940
[S28] And so our whole factory now runs on this object-oriented factory and quality system.

973
01:01:26.940 --> 01:01:30.760
[S28] The last generation of, our latest generation of robots, which we've deployed this year,

974
01:01:30.760 --> 01:01:33.059
[S28] we actually built the hardware.

975
01:01:33.059 --> 01:01:36.940
[S28] I've been to Japan maybe, you know, a lot of times, maybe 30, 40 times.

976
01:01:36.940 --> 01:01:38.670
[S28] And I love to tour factories over there.

977
01:01:38.670 --> 01:01:42.269
[S28] And I, they always amaze me because they built everything themselves.

978
01:01:42.269 --> 01:01:43.630
[S28] They weren't afraid of anything.

979
01:01:43.630 --> 01:01:46.920
[S28] They needed a robot, they'd try to buy one, but if they couldn't, they'd actually engineer

980
01:01:46.920 --> 01:01:47.519
[S28] it and build it.

981
01:01:47.519 --> 01:01:49.369
[S28] And you'd think this was really expensive.

982
01:01:49.369 --> 01:01:52.389
[S28] But we found out it's pretty cheap.

983
01:01:52.389 --> 01:01:54.230
[S28] It's actually cheaper than buying them.

984
01:01:54.230 --> 01:01:57.239
[S28] And so we've actually now designed or specced out our own robots.

985
01:01:57.239 --> 01:02:00.239
[S28] We don't mill the metal or anything, we get that all made, we put them all together.

986
01:02:00.239 --> 01:02:04.489
[S28] And we do the software top to bottom, and we have now some extraordinarily advanced

987
01:02:04.489 --> 01:02:06.010
[S28] robots in the factory.

988
01:02:06.010 --> 01:02:11.710
[S28] And our computers are built, start to finish, on the key components, completely untouched

989
01:02:11.710 --> 01:02:13.510
[S28] by human hands.

990
01:02:13.510 --> 01:02:17.210
[S28] So we're pretty convinced we're the low-cost producer.

991
01:02:17.210 --> 01:02:21.210
[S28] We do it in Fremont, California, right under our nose.

992
01:02:21.210 --> 01:02:24.989
[S28] And we export them to Japan and all sorts of other places.

993
01:02:24.989 --> 01:02:30.900
[S28] And Canon is our partner in Japan, and they, they do very, very thorough quality audits.

994
01:02:30.900 --> 01:02:34.739
[S28] And we're now at the point where we're directly shipped a stock with them, and they, they

995
01:02:34.739 --> 01:02:37.679
[S28] say we're a very high-quality supplier.

996
01:02:37.679 --> 01:02:38.179
[S28] So...

997
01:02:38.179 --> 01:02:42.570
[S29] How do your lines tie in with your research and your development team?

998
01:02:42.570 --> 01:02:47.070
[S29] Because I've heard that they could actually change the line from their own computers.

999
01:02:47.070 --> 01:02:47.989
[S28] Yeah, they can.

1000
01:02:47.989 --> 01:02:51.599
[S28] Well, we don't give everybody permission to do that, but yes, they can.

1001
01:02:51.599 --> 01:02:52.679
[S28] Here's how it works.

1002
01:02:52.679 --> 01:02:56.880
[S28] One of the things we do is we actually, when we want to build an engineering prototype,

1003
01:02:56.880 --> 01:03:02.070
[S28] see, what happens in most, one of the key things that manufacturing can contribute

1004
01:03:02.070 --> 01:03:04.349
[S28] to competitive advantage is time to market.

1005
01:03:04.349 --> 01:03:05.400
[S28] Why is that?

1006
01:03:05.400 --> 01:03:12.599
[S28] Because the way most things work is you design your product here, and after you're done,

1007
01:03:12.599 --> 01:03:18.070
[S28] you throw it over the wall, and you design your manufacturing process here, right?

1008
01:03:18.070 --> 01:03:20.989
[S28] Sorting out a bunch of things that maybe weren't done right here, fixing them, changing

1009
01:03:20.989 --> 01:03:23.570
[S28] them, and then completing the process design.

1010
01:03:23.570 --> 01:03:28.210
[S28] What you want to do is do this and ship it right here while your competitors are still

1011
01:03:28.210 --> 01:03:29.289
[S28] here.

1012
01:03:29.289 --> 01:03:32.099
[S28] And that's what we've been able to do in many cases.

1013
01:03:32.099 --> 01:03:37.179
[S28] What we do is we suck data out of our CAD systems in engineering.

1014
01:03:37.179 --> 01:03:40.599
[S28] We zing them around over the local networks, over a T1 to Fremont, the factory's about

1015
01:03:40.599 --> 01:03:42.130
[S28] 15 minutes away.

1016
01:03:42.130 --> 01:03:49.570
[S28] And in our own computers, we compute all of the robot placement programs, fully optimized

1017
01:03:49.570 --> 01:03:50.849
[S28] path.

1018
01:03:50.849 --> 01:03:55.260
[S28] We compute all the vision system programs, we check it against the bill of materials

1019
01:03:55.260 --> 01:04:00.570
[S28] in the IS system, and we download it to the robots, and we're ready to build a board,

1020
01:04:00.570 --> 01:04:05.389
[S28] lot size of one, in between two production CPU boards on the line.

1021
01:04:05.389 --> 01:04:08.559
[S28] Full surface mount with all of our automation technology.

1022
01:04:50.670 --> 01:04:54.230
[S30] A lot of times, when you build prototypes,

1023
01:04:54.260 --> 01:04:57.829
[S30] it's not quite the same technology as you're going to use in production.

1024
01:04:57.860 --> 01:05:01.329
[S30] And so, all the accumulated knowledge you get from building your prototypes,

1025
01:05:01.360 --> 01:05:04.769
[S30] you throw away when you change technology to go into production.

1026
01:05:04.800 --> 01:05:07.369
[S30] And you start over in that accumulation process.

1027
01:05:07.400 --> 01:05:10.530
[S30] Because we don't change technology,

1028
01:05:10.559 --> 01:05:15.610
[S30] we don't throw anything away, we don't waste time.

1029
01:05:15.639 --> 01:05:18.630
[S30] And it's led to one of the healthiest relationships

1030
01:05:18.659 --> 01:05:22.170
[S30] between an engineering and manufacturing group I've ever seen in my life.

1031
01:05:22.199 --> 01:05:24.269
[S30] They're all working off the same databases,

1032
01:05:24.289 --> 01:05:26.809
[S30] they're all working on the same processes,

1033
01:05:26.840 --> 01:05:30.409
[S30] they're all working in a very disciplined process environment

1034
01:05:30.429 --> 01:05:32.630
[S30] to where, when any processes are changed,

1035
01:05:32.659 --> 01:05:36.090
[S30] they all get together and review the proposals,

1036
01:05:36.119 --> 01:05:37.329
[S30] and all buy into it.

1037
01:05:37.360 --> 01:05:39.449
[S30] And it's not that hard.

1038
01:05:39.480 --> 01:05:42.329
[S30] The key to it all, though, was we didn't go out

1039
01:05:42.360 --> 01:05:45.010
[S30] and hire a bunch of manufacturing people.

1040
01:05:45.039 --> 01:05:47.449
[S30] We went out and hired engineers,

1041
01:05:47.480 --> 01:05:50.210
[S30] and we convinced them that we were going to be different,

1042
01:05:50.239 --> 01:05:52.050
[S30] we were going to pay them exactly the same.

1043
01:05:52.079 --> 01:05:54.610
[S30] As a matter of fact, we paid them a little more at the beginning.

1044
01:05:54.639 --> 01:05:57.929
[S30] But we pay them exactly the same as R&amp;D.

1045
01:05:57.949 --> 01:05:58.679
[S30] No different.

1046
01:05:58.699 --> 01:06:01.449
[S30] There's migration both directions.

1047
01:06:01.480 --> 01:06:05.369
[S30] Not just from manufacturing into R&amp;D, but both directions.

1048
01:06:05.400 --> 01:06:07.730
[S30] And they're not second-class citizens.

1049
01:06:07.760 --> 01:06:10.650
[S30] They have the same offices, they have the same test equipment,

1050
01:06:10.679 --> 01:06:14.650
[S30] they have the same computers on their desks.

1051
01:06:14.679 --> 01:06:18.530
[S30] And it took us a while to convince them that we were really serious.

1052
01:06:18.559 --> 01:06:21.010
[S30] Until, for about the first few years,

1053
01:06:21.039 --> 01:06:23.809
[S30] we had more PhDs in manufacturing than we did design engineering,

1054
01:06:23.840 --> 01:06:26.409
[S30] until design engineering stole a few of them away.

1055
01:06:26.440 --> 01:06:28.650
[S30] So, it's really paid off for us,

1056
01:06:28.679 --> 01:06:34.010
[S30] and I think it's one of our real opportunities for competitive advantage.

1057
01:06:34.039 --> 01:06:36.849
[S30] Yeah, I think one or two more, and we've got to run.

1058
01:06:36.880 --> 01:06:37.769
[S30] Probably you do too.

1059
01:06:37.800 --> 01:06:38.929
[S30] Yes, up there, Ty.

1060
01:06:38.960 --> 01:06:39.929
[S31] Do you have no warehouses?

1061
01:06:39.960 --> 01:06:41.369
[S31] Is that a measure?

1062
01:06:41.400 --> 01:06:43.409
[S31] You are the true JIT?

1063
01:06:43.440 --> 01:06:43.929
[S30] Yeah.

1064
01:06:43.960 --> 01:06:46.570
[S31] Who's, how are you getting your products?

1065
01:06:46.599 --> 01:06:48.409
[S31] Or your raw materials?

1066
01:06:48.440 --> 01:06:50.489
[S30] How are we getting our raw materials?

1067
01:06:50.519 --> 01:06:53.010
[S30] You mean, like, what truck line brings them in?

1068
01:06:53.039 --> 01:06:53.809
[S30] Or what do you mean?

1069
01:06:53.840 --> 01:06:54.610
[S31] I don't know.

1070
01:06:54.639 --> 01:06:58.570
[S31] Is it air, is it truck?

1071
01:06:58.599 --> 01:07:00.730
[S31] I mean, how are you getting everything up quickly?

1072
01:07:00.760 --> 01:07:03.849
[S30] See, the key thing is, that's not our problem.

1073
01:07:03.880 --> 01:07:05.929
[S30] That's our supplier's problem.

1074
01:07:05.960 --> 01:07:07.849
[S30] So, we agree with our supplier

1075
01:07:07.880 --> 01:07:11.329
[S30] when the stuff is going to arrive on our factory floor.

1076
01:07:11.360 --> 01:07:15.730
[S30] And if they can, if they're together enough to ship it by truck,

1077
01:07:15.760 --> 01:07:16.519
[S30] that's fine.

1078
01:07:16.550 --> 01:07:20.210
[S30] If they have to ship it by air, that's too bad.

1079
01:07:20.239 --> 01:07:22.369
[S30] If they want to have a warehouse next to ours

1080
01:07:22.400 --> 01:07:25.849
[S30] because they're not good enough, well, then they have to do that.

1081
01:07:25.880 --> 01:07:29.489
[S30] Now, we're not giants, so we can't go command people to do things.

1082
01:07:29.519 --> 01:07:33.409
[S30] But what's happened is, is we have a fairly narrow supply base.

1083
01:07:33.440 --> 01:07:36.409
[S30] We don't have 3 billion suppliers.

1084
01:07:36.440 --> 01:07:39.610
[S30] And they see tremendous advantages in working with us.

1085
01:07:39.639 --> 01:07:42.530
[S30] We're pushing our quality information systems back to them.

1086
01:07:42.559 --> 01:07:44.409
[S30] As an example, Motorola is one of our key suppliers.

1087
01:07:44.440 --> 01:07:47.130
[S30] Almost every key supplier has NeXT computers,

1088
01:07:47.159 --> 01:07:50.329
[S30] and we send them statistical quality information,

1089
01:07:50.360 --> 01:07:52.849
[S30] sometimes daily.

1090
01:07:52.880 --> 01:07:53.769
[S30] Daily.

1091
01:07:53.800 --> 01:07:57.489
[S30] Off our automated quality information systems on their parts.

1092
01:07:57.519 --> 01:08:00.809
[S30] And those kinds of things are extremely valuable to them.

1093
01:08:00.840 --> 01:08:03.530
[S30] So, while we're not Goliath,

1094
01:08:03.559 --> 01:08:06.170
[S30] we're a very valuable David to work with.

1095
01:08:06.199 --> 01:08:08.889
[S30] And so, they really bend over backwards to work with us.

1096
01:08:08.920 --> 01:08:13.329
[S30] And we try to push the problems where they belong.

1097
01:08:13.360 --> 01:08:16.130
[S30] If it's our problems, we take full responsibility for them.

1098
01:08:16.159 --> 01:08:17.890
[S30] We own our process.

1099
01:08:17.920 --> 01:08:22.729
[S30] But they, it's their job to get us zero defect material

1100
01:08:22.760 --> 01:08:25.050
[S30] on time, per agreements.

1101
01:08:25.079 --> 01:08:28.130
[S30] And our philosophy is, our money doesn't break after we give it to them,

1102
01:08:28.159 --> 01:08:30.010
[S30] so their parts shouldn't break after they give them to us.

1103
01:08:30.039 --> 01:08:39.609
[S30] Yeah.

1104
01:08:39.640 --> 01:08:44.609
[S31] Do you see the possibility of bringing NeXT over to 64 Macs?

1105
01:08:44.640 --> 01:08:46.529
[S31] And do you see a future path to that?

1106
01:08:46.560 --> 01:08:49.729
[S31] Does it seem dying out or just, um?

1107
01:08:49.760 --> 01:08:52.449
[S30] Why, you know, I think Macs are going to continue to grow.
